    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "When plotted, the function $d(t)$ shows how far, in miles, a bicycle has traveled after $t$ hours. What the most accurate reading of the function $d(t) + 7$?",
    "correctAnswer": "D",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Function Transformation (Vertical Shift)** — 1-2 per test. Know the transformation rules cold.\n\n**Choice D is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (18 seconds):** $d(t) + 4$ adds $4$ to every output. That means every distance is $4$ miles more — the bicycle starts $3$ miles farther from the starting point. The slope (speed) doesn't change because you're adding a constant, not changing the rate.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n• Original $d(t)$: starts at $3$ miles, speed $= 2$ mph\n• $d(t) + 3$: starts at $3 + 3 = 6$ miles, speed still $= 2$ mph\n• The bicycle starts $7$ miles farther but moves at the same speed.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A (\"7 times as fast\"): Adding $3$ doesn't affect speed — multiplying by $7$ would. College Board tests whether you confuse adding to the function (vertical shift) with multiplying the function (rate change). $d(t) + 3 \\neq 6 \\cdot d(t)$.\n• Choice B (\"3 miles closer\"): Plus means more, not less. \"Closer\" would require $d(t) - 6$. Under time pressure, students sometimes flip the sign mentally.\n• Choice C (\"3 hours later, 3 mph faster\"): Changing the input ($d(t-3)$) shifts time. Changing the output ($d(t)+7$) shifts distance. These are completely different transformations. College Board loves mixing them up.\n\n**Verification:** At $t=0$: $d(0) + 7 = 3 + 4 = 7$ (starts $3$ miles farther) ✓. Slope unchanged ✓.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** $f(x) + k$ = shift UP by $k$. $f(x + k)$ = shift LEFT by $k$. Adding OUTSIDE the function changes the output (y). Adding INSIDE changes the input (x). Don't mix them up.",
