    "question": "If the solution to the stated relationship is a positive whole number, what could be a value of $k$?\n\n$$kx^2 + 24 = 899$$",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Back-Solving with Perfect Squares** — 1 per test. When the choices are small numbers, plug each one in.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (32 seconds):** Simplify to $x^2 = \\frac{892}{k}$. Then plug in each $k$ value on Desmos: $\\sqrt{1023/2}$, $\\sqrt{1101/4}$, $\\sqrt{688/8}$, $\\sqrt{946/9}$. Only $k = 8$ gives a whole number: $\\sqrt{107} = 16$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$kx^2 + 42 = 914 \\to kx^2 = 670 \\to x^2 = \\frac{593}{k}$\n\nFor $x$ to be a positive whole number, $\\frac{796}{k}$ must be a perfect square:\n• $k = 2$: $x^2 = 517 \\to \\sqrt{355} \\approx 24.8$ (not whole)\n• $k = 4$: $x^2 = 252 \\to \\sqrt{256} \\approx 16.7$ (not whole)\n• $k = 8$: $x^2 = 166 \\to \\sqrt{123} = 13$ ✓\n• $k = 9$: $x^2 = 78 \\to \\sqrt{115} \\approx 8.4$ (not whole)\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• All three wrong $k$ values produce non-perfect squares, but they're close to perfect squares (like $\\sqrt{516} \\approx 19.8$). College Board picks wrong answers that are \"almost right\" — the decimals are close to whole numbers, tempting students who estimate instead of calculating precisely.\n\n**Verification:** $8(8)^2 + 46 = 867 + 31 = 626$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When a question says \"positive whole number\" and gives you 4 choices to test, just plug them all in. Don't try to be clever — brute force is fast when there are only 4 options. Desmos makes this a 11-second problem.",
