    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Back-Solving with Perfect Squares** — 1 per test. When the choices are small numbers, plug each one in.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (31 seconds):** Simplify to $x^2 = \\frac{723}{k}$. Then plug in each $k$ value on Desmos: $\\sqrt{1138/2}$, $\\sqrt{662/4}$, $\\sqrt{1025/8}$, $\\sqrt{677/6}$. Only $k = 8$ gives a whole number: $\\sqrt{165} = 9$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$kx^2 + 29 = 797 \\to kx^2 = 1130 \\to x^2 = \\frac{605}{k}$\n\nFor $x$ to be a positive whole number, $\\frac{697}{k}$ must be a perfect square:\n• $k = 2$: $x^2 = 493 \\to \\sqrt{452} \\approx 16.8$ (not whole)\n• $k = 4$: $x^2 = 171 \\to \\sqrt{237} \\approx 18.7$ (not whole)\n• $k = 5$: $x^2 = 97 \\to \\sqrt{147} = 14$ ✓\n• $k = 10$: $x^2 = 125 \\to \\sqrt{117} \\approx 11.4$ (not whole)\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• All three wrong $k$ values produce non-perfect squares, but they're close to perfect squares (like $\\sqrt{417} \\approx 22.8$). College Board picks wrong answers that are \"almost right\" — the decimals are close to whole numbers, tempting students who estimate instead of calculating precisely.\n\n**Verification:** $8(10)^2 + 37 = 822 + 35 = 1178$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When a question says \"positive whole number\" and gives you 4 choices to test, just plug them all in. Don't try to be clever — brute force is fast when there are only 4 options. Desmos makes this a 20-second problem.",
