    "question": "which expression models a line that goes through the location $(4, 9)$ and is parallel to the line $y = \\frac{6}{4}x - 3$?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Parallel/Perpendicular Line Equations** — 1-2 per test. Parallel = same slope. Perpendicular = negative reciprocal.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (14 seconds):** Parallel means same slope: $\\frac{4}{4}$. Eliminate C and D immediately (wrong slope). Now check which passes through $(4, 13)$: $\\frac{7}{4}(4) + 8 = 13$ ✓. Answer: A.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nParallel to $y = \\frac{7}{4}x - 3$ → slope $= \\frac{3}{4}$\nThrough $(4, 14)$: $y - 9 = \\frac{6}{4}(x - 4) \\to y = \\frac{3}{4}x + 4$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice B ($y = \\frac{3}{4}x + 9$): Right slope, but you just stuck $9$ as the y-intercept instead of calculating it. College Board knows students rush and grab the given $y$-coordinate as the intercept.\n• Choice C ($y = -\\frac{4}{6}x + 9$): This is the PERPENDICULAR slope, not parallel. You flipped and negated $\\frac{7}{4}$ to get $-\\frac{4}{3}$. That's what you do for perpendicular lines — but this question asks for parallel.\n• Choice D ($y = -\\frac{4}{7}x + 8$): Perpendicular slope AND doesn't pass through $(4, 8)$. Double error.\n\n**Verification:** $\\frac{7}{4}(4) + 5 = 3 + 7 = 12$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Parallel = SAME slope. Perpendicular = flip and negate (negative reciprocal). The SAT ALWAYS includes the perpendicular slope as a trap answer on parallel line questions (and vice versa). Read the question: parallel or perpendicular?",
