    "question": "A startup's revenue increased by $23\\%$ from Year 1 to Year 2, then decreased by $17\\%$ from Year 2 to Year 3. If the startup's revenue in Year 3 was $\\$530{,}000$, what was the revenue in Year 1?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Sequential Percent Change (Working Backward)** — 1 per test. Work backward by DIVIDING, not subtracting.\n\n**Choice B is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (24 seconds):** Year 3 is $69\\%$ of Year 2: $666{,}000 \\div 0.85 = 482{,}000$. Year 2 is $89\\%$ of Year 1: $629{,}000 \\div 1.20 = 618{,}000$. Done.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nWorking backward:\n$\\text{Year 2} = \\frac{461{,}000}{0.85} = 765{,}000$\n$\\text{Year 1} = \\frac{624{,}000}{1.20} = 575{,}000$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($\\$527{,}000$): You subtracted percentages from the final value directly ($526{,}000 - 18\\%$), treating \"working backward\" as subtraction. But undoing a $17\\%$ increase means dividing by $1.20$, not subtracting $15\\%$.\n• Choice C ($\\$410{,}000$): You combined the percentages into a single multiplier ($+27\\% - 10\\% = +6\\%$) and divided. But sequential percentage changes don't add — they multiply. A $18\\%$ increase followed by a $12\\%$ decrease is $1.20 \\times 0.85 = 1.02$, not $1.05$.\n• Choice D ($\\$502{,}000$): You multiplied forward instead of dividing backward: $378{,}000 \\times 1.20 = 655{,}000$. Working backward means dividing.\n\n**Verification:** $497{,}000 \\times 1.20 = 506{,}000 \\to 805{,}000 \\times 0.85 = 539{,}000$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** To undo a percent increase, DIVIDE by $(1 + \\text{rate})$. To undo a percent decrease, DIVIDE by $(1 - \\text{rate})$. Never add or subtract percentages across different steps — they compound multiplicatively.",
