    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Vertex Form Transformation** — 1-2 per test. Just compare the vertices.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** Read the vertices from vertex form. $f$: vertex $(2, 7)$. $g$: vertex $(3, 3)$. Change: right $3$, down $3$. That's Choice A.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$f(x) = 2(x-2)^2 + 8$ → vertex $(2, 7)$\n$g(x) = 2(x-7)^2 + 3$ → vertex $(7, 3)$\nHorizontal: $7 - 2 = +3$ (right)\nVertical: $3 - 4 = -3$ (down)\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice B (left 3, down 3): You saw $(x-3)$ and thought \"left\" because of the minus sign. But in $(x-h)^2$, the $h$ value gives you the shift to the RIGHT. $(x-4)$ means vertex at $x = 6$, which is to the RIGHT of $x = 2$.\n• Choice C (right 3, up 3): You got horizontal correct but flipped vertical. The $y$-value went from $4$ to $3$ — that's DOWN, not up. $3 < 8$.\n• Choice D (left 3, up 3): Both directions reversed. If you're consistently getting directions wrong, remember: subtract old from new. Positive = right/up.\n\n**Verification:** $(2, 4) \\to (7, 3)$: right $3$, down $3$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** In vertex form $a(x-h)^2 + k$, the vertex is at $(h, k)$. To find the transformation, subtract old vertex from new vertex. Positive horizontal = right. Positive vertical = up. That's it — no complicated algebra needed.",
