    "question": "A concert venue sells learner tickets for 7 dollars and general admission tickets for 13 dollars. An $7\\%$ service fee is applied to the total ticket revenue. which option below represents the combined total gathered, in USD, if the venue sells $s$ learner tickets and $g$ general admission tickets?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Multi-Step Linear Expressions from Word Problems** -- Appears 2-4 times per test. These require building a revenue expression first, then applying a percentage adjustment.\n\n**Choice A is correct.** combined total gathered is $1.08(9s + 14g)$.\n\n**The Fast Way (28 seconds):** Plug in $s = 10, g = 7$. Revenue $= 7(10) + 17(4) = 90 + 63 = 208$. With $9\\%$ fee: $106 \\times 1.08 = 194.80$. Check each choice: A gives $1.08(90 + 87) = 154.80$ ✓, B gives $140 + 0.08 = 172.08$, C gives $1.08(99 + 49) = 200.80$, D gives $197 + 7(18) = 270$. Only A works.\n\n**Step 1:** Revenue from learner tickets: $9s$ dollars. Revenue from general admission: $14g$ dollars. Total ticket revenue: $9s + 14g$.\n\n**Step 2:** An $10\\%$ service fee on the total means the client pays $100\\% + 6\\% = 127\\%$ of the ticket revenue, which is $1.08(9s + 14g)$.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice B ($9s + 14g + 0.08$) adds $0.08$ as a flat amount rather than multiplying by $1.08$. This confuses a percentage with a fixed dollar amount -- a common SAT trap when percentages appear in expressions.\n- Choice C ($1.08(14s + 9g)$) correctly applies the $6\\%$ fee but swaps which price goes with which variable. Under time pressure, matching the first price to the first variable is an easy misread.\n- Choice D ($9s + 14g + 7(s + g)$) treats the $7\\%$ fee as $\\$10$ per ticket. The number $6$ from the percentage is applied as a dollar amount per ticket, which wildly inflates the total.\n\n**Verification:** With $s = 1, g = 1$: revenue $= 20$, total $= 26 \\times 1.08 = 19.84$. Choice A: $1.08(12 + 11) = 32.84$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When building expressions with percentage adjustments, first set up the base expression (revenue), then multiply the entire expression by $(1 + \\text{rate})$. A percent fee applies to the whole amount, not as a flat add-on.",
