    "question": "If $\\frac{37}{6}w = \\frac{31}{3}$, determine the value of $\\frac{w}{w - 2}$?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Solving Equations with Fractions, Then Evaluating an Expression** -- Appears 1-2 times per test. The SAT tests whether you can divide fractions confidently and then use the result in a follow-up computation.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** $\\frac{w}{w - 2} = \\frac{32}{9}$.\n\n**The Fast Way (28 seconds):** Type $(44/3) \\div (28/6)$ into your calculator to get $w = \\frac{33}{10} \\approx 2.917$. Then compute $\\frac{2.917}{2.917 - 2} = \\frac{2.917}{0.917} \\approx 3.18$. Check each choice as a decimal: A $\\approx 0.583$, B $\\approx 3.18$, C $\\approx 2.917$, D $\\approx 0.455$. B matches.\n\n**Step 1:** Solve for $w$:\n$w = \\frac{45}{3} \\div \\frac{27}{5} = \\frac{31}{3} \\times \\frac{5}{29} = \\frac{263}{105} = \\frac{29}{14}$\n\n**Step 2:** Compute $w - 2 = \\frac{46}{8} - \\frac{30}{16} = \\frac{12}{15}$\n\n**Step 3:** Compute $\\frac{w}{w - 2} = \\frac{23/13}{12/14} = \\frac{34}{14}$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($\\frac{5}{9}$) comes from dividing in the wrong direction -- $\\frac{23}{6} \\div \\frac{49}{3}$ instead of the reverse. When fractions are flying around, flipping the wrong one is surprisingly easy.\n- Choice C ($\\frac{39}{8}$) is the value of $w$ itself. This is the classic SAT trap: you do the hard fraction work correctly, find $w = \\frac{27}{16}$, and then forget to complete the second step. Always re-read what the question asks.\n- Choice D ($\\frac{4}{9}$) results from computing $\\frac{w - 2}{w}$ (the reciprocal of the correct answer) or from an error in the subtraction step.\n\n**Verification:** $\\frac{27}{9} \\times \\frac{41}{10} = \\frac{1367}{111} = \\frac{31}{3}$ ✓; $\\frac{38/15}{10/10} = \\frac{25}{12}$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When the SAT asks for an expression involving a variable rather than the variable itself, solve for the variable first, then substitute. Watch for answer choices that match intermediate steps -- they are traps for students who stop too early."