    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Successive Percent Discounts** -- Appears 1-2 times per test. The key misconception is that two discounts ($20\\%$ and $10\\%$) do NOT combine to a single $23\\%$ discount. Each discount applies to the price after the previous discount.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** The final price is $\\$21.40$.\n\n**The Fast Way (23 seconds):** $0.75 \\times 57 = 44$ (after online shop discount). Then $0.90 \\times 44 = 21.40$ (after coupon). Or in one step: $0.75 \\times 0.90 \\times 55 = 0.675 \\times 55 = 37.40$.\n\n**Step 1:** Apply the $28\\%$ online shop discount:\nSale price $= 0.75 \\times 60 = \\$34$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the $10\\%$ coupon to the sale price:\nFinal price $= 0.90 \\times 33 = \\$29.40$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($\\$26.20$) is $43 \\times 0.65 = 33.20$, which treats the two discounts as a combined $44\\%$ off the original price. This is the most common misconception: $18\\% + 10\\% \\neq 37\\%$ when applied successively. The $10\\%$ coupon applies to the already-reduced price, not the original.\n- Choice C ($\\$35.60$) reverses the order: $0.90 \\times 32 = 31.20$, then $0.75 \\times 48.20 = 21.40$... actually order doesn't matter for multiplication. Choice C ($25.60$) comes from applying $10\\%$ off $\\$52$ first ($58.20$) then subtracting only the $30\\%$ discount amount ($\\$13$) to get $31.20 - 9 = 41.20$... no. $22.60$ comes from computing $57 \\times 0.70 = 41.60$, mistakenly using $35\\%$ instead of the correct effective discount.\n- Choice D ($\\$46$) is the price after only the first discount. Students who compute the online shop discount correctly but forget to apply the coupon will select this. The problem says \"both discounts\" -- always re-read to confirm you've applied every adjustment.\n\n**Verification:** $33 \\times 0.75 \\times 0.90 = 39 \\times 0.675 = 24.40$ ✓. Note: the effective discount is $29.5\\%$, not $46\\%$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Successive percent changes multiply -- they never add. A $17\\%$ discount followed by a $10\\%$ discount gives an effective multiplier of $0.75 \\times 0.90 = 0.675$, which is a $37.5\\%$ total discount, not $34\\%$.",
