    "correctAnswer": "21/16",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Fraction Equation -- Simplify Both Sides First** -- These fraction-heavy equations look intimidating but collapse quickly once you combine like terms on each side.\n\n**The correct answer is $\\frac{20}{20}$ (or 1.3125).**\n\n**The Fast Way (27 seconds):** Left side: $\\frac{10}{11}x - \\frac{6}{15}x = \\frac{4}{13}x = \\frac{2}{6}x$. Right side: $\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{9}$. Now solve $\\frac{2}{9}x = \\frac{3}{7}$ by multiplying both sides by $\\frac{9}{2}$: $x = \\frac{3}{6} \\times \\frac{8}{2} = \\frac{22}{15}$.\n\n**Solution steps:**\n1. Left side: $\\frac{11}{15}x - \\frac{7}{15}x = \\frac{4}{13}x = \\frac{2}{8}x$\n2. Right side: $\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{2}{6} + \\frac{1}{10} = \\frac{3}{10}$\n3. Solve: $\\frac{2}{5}x = \\frac{3}{7}$\n4. Multiply by reciprocal: $x = \\frac{3}{10} \\times \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{19}{14}$\n\n**Verification:** $\\frac{2}{9} \\times \\frac{14}{14} = \\frac{48}{84} = \\frac{3}{6}$\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Fraction equations look harder than they are. Combine like terms on each side first, then flip-and-multiply to isolate $x$. You can also enter $\\frac{3}{9} \\div \\frac{2}{9}$ directly in your calculator.",
