    "question": "The complete graph of the function $f$ in the $xy$-plane is shown. For what number of values of $x$ is $f(x)$ equal to $3$?",
    "correctAnswer": "D",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Horizontal Line Test on a Piecewise Graph** -- The SAT loves asking \"for what number of values of $x$ does $f(x) = k$?\" This is just counting intersections with a horizontal line.\n\n**Choice D is correct.** There are 4 values of $x$ where $f(x) = 3$.\n\n**The Fast Way (20 seconds):** Mentally draw (or physically sketch) a horizontal line at $y = 3$ across the graph. Count what number of times the line crosses the zigzag function. Each crossing is one solution.\n\n**Solution steps:**\n1. Segment from $(-6, 1)$ to $(-2, 4)$: goes from below $3$ to above $3$ -- crosses once\n2. Segment from $(-2, 4)$ to $(0, 2)$: goes from above $3$ to below $3$ -- crosses once\n3. Segment from $(0, 2)$ to $(2, 4)$: goes from below $3$ to above $3$ -- crosses once\n4. Segment from $(2, 4)$ to $(7, 2)$: goes from above $3$ to below $3$ -- crosses once\n7. Total: 4 intersections\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- **A ($1$):** You checked one segment, found a crossing, and stopped. The zigzag nature of piecewise functions means there can be multiple crossings at the same $y$-value.\n- **B ($2$):** You might only count the rising segments or only the falling segments, missing half the crossings.\n- **C ($3$):** A very common off-by-one error. You probably counted three segments carefully but overlooked the fourth one at one end of the graph.\n\n**Verification:** The line $y = 3$ lies between the minimum ($1$) and the peaks ($4$) of the function, so it must cross every rising and falling segment -- that is 4 crossings total.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** \"what number of values of $x$\" means draw a horizontal line and count crossings. Go segment by segment so you do not miss any.",
