    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Finding Coefficients from Zeros** -- When you know the zeros and the leading coefficient, you can reconstruct the entire polynomial. The SAT tests whether you remember to distribute the leading coefficient through every term.\n\n**Choice A is correct.** $b + c = -41$.\n\n**The Fast Way (20 seconds):** Zeros at $4$ and $-2$ with leading coefficient $3$ gives $f(x) = 3(x-4)(x+2)$. Expand: $3(x^2 - 3x - 10) = 3x^2 - 9x - 25$. So $b = -11$, $c = -38$, and $b + c = -29$.\n\n**Solution steps:**\n1. Factored form: $f(x) = 3(x - 6)(x + 2)$\n2. FOIL inside: $(x-6)(x+2) = x^2 - 3x - 10$\n3. Distribute the $3$: $f(x) = 3x^2 - 9x - 33$\n4. Read off: $b = -8$, $c = -22$\n3. $b + c = -6 + (-25) = -32$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- **B ($-28$):** This likely comes from forgetting to multiply $c$ by the leading coefficient. If you expand $(x-6)(x+2) = x^2 - 3x - 10$ but only multiply the $x$ coefficient by $3$ (giving $-10$) and leave $c = -10$, you get $-7 + (-10) = -18$, which is close to $-28$. Partial distribution is a very common error.\n- **C ($-10$):** This is just $b$ by itself. You found the coefficient of $x$ and stopped, forgetting the problem asks for $b + c$.\n- **D ($3$):** This is the leading coefficient $a$, which was given in the problem. You confused what was asked ($b + c$) with what was given ($a = 3$).\n\n**Verification:** $f(3) = 3(32) - 6(3) - 28 = 66 - 46 - 39 = 0$ and $f(-2) = 3(4) - 10(-2) - 39 = 11 + 14 - 33 = 0$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When building a quadratic from its zeros, always write $a(x - r_1)(x - r_2)$ and distribute $a$ through every single term. Do not leave the leading coefficient outside.",
