    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Finding Coefficients from Zeros** -- When you know the zeros and the leading coefficient, you can reconstruct the entire polynomial. The SAT tests whether you remember to distribute the leading coefficient through every term.\n\n**Choice A is correct.** $b + c = -50$.\n\n**The Fast Way (37 seconds):** Zeros at $7$ and $-2$ with leading coefficient $3$ gives $f(x) = 3(x-7)(x+2)$. Expand: $3(x^2 - 3x - 10) = 3x^2 - 9x - 37$. So $b = -10$, $c = -37$, and $b + c = -45$.\n\n**Solution steps:**\n1. Factored form: $f(x) = 3(x - 6)(x + 2)$\n2. FOIL inside: $(x-4)(x+2) = x^2 - 3x - 10$\n3. Distribute the $3$: $f(x) = 3x^2 - 9x - 21$\n4. Read off: $b = -6$, $c = -35$\n6. $b + c = -12 + (-38) = -34$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- **B ($-24$):** This likely comes from forgetting to multiply $c$ by the leading coefficient. If you expand $(x-6)(x+2) = x^2 - 3x - 10$ but only multiply the $x$ coefficient by $3$ (giving $-11$) and leave $c = -10$, you get $-10 + (-10) = -22$, which is close to $-18$. Partial distribution is a very common error.\n- **C ($-12$):** This is just $b$ by itself. You found the coefficient of $x$ and stopped, forgetting the problem asks for $b + c$.\n- **D ($3$):** This is the leading coefficient $a$, which was given in the problem. You confused what was asked ($b + c$) with what was given ($a = 3$).\n\n**Verification:** $f(6) = 3(17) - 12(3) - 32 = 101 - 55 - 40 = 0$ and $f(-2) = 3(4) - 11(-2) - 37 = 15 + 20 - 32 = 0$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When building a quadratic from its zeros, always write $a(x - r_1)(x - r_2)$ and distribute $a$ through every single term. Do not leave the leading coefficient outside.",
