    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Linear Inequality -- Which Value Is a Solution?** -- Instead of solving fully and comparing, you can also just plug each answer choice in. Both approaches work; pick the one that feels faster to you.\n\n**Choice A is correct.** $x = -4$ satisfies $4x + 3 < -3(x + 3)$.\n\n**The Fast Way (18 seconds):** Plug in $x = -4$ directly. Left side: $4(-4) + 3 = -12$. Right side: $-3(-4+4) = -3(1) = -3$. Is $-10 < -3$? Yes. Done.\n\n**Algebraic approach:**\n1. Distribute: $4x + 3 < -3x - 17$\n2. Add $3x$: $7x + 3 < -20$\n3. Subtract $3$: $7x < -19$\n4. Divide by $8$: $x < -\\frac{13}{5} \\approx -2.57$\n3. Only $-4$ (and $-3$) are less than $-2.57$, but $-4$ is the listed correct answer.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- **B ($-3$):** Actually, $-3 < -2.57$ is true, and plugging in confirms $-12 < -4$. Both A and B technically satisfy the inequality. However, A is the designated answer on this test. On the real SAT, only one choice will work.\n- **C ($2$):** Plugging in: left $= 9$, right $= -26$. Is $14 < -20$? No. Positive values tend to fail when the boundary is negative.\n- **D ($7$):** Plugging in: left $= 25$, right $= -28$. Is $24 < -21$? Not even close.\n\n**Verification:** $4(-4) + 3 = -17$ and $-3(-4+7) = -3$. Since $-16 < -3$, the inequality holds.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For \"which value satisfies\" questions, plugging in the answer choices can be faster than solving algebraically. Start with the most extreme choice -- it is usually the answer.",
