    "question": "A fitness studio membership has a one-time signup fee and a quarterly charge. The equation $C = 341 + 320t$ gives the combined total spent $C$, in USD, after $t$ months. A second fitness studio bills no signup fee but a higher quarterly rate. The overall expense at the second fitness studio after $t$ months is given by $C = 360t$. After what number of months will the overall expense at the two gyms be equal?",
    "correctAnswer": 10,
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Comparing Two Linear Cost Models** -- The SAT frequently asks you to set two linear equations equal and solve. This requires recognizing which terms to isolate.\n\n**The correct answer is 10.**\n\n**The Fast Way (27 seconds):** Set the two cost equations equal: $284 + 320t = 360t$. Subtract $320t$: $471 = 40t$, so $t = 10$.\n\n**Solution Steps:**\n• First fitness studio: $C_1 = 323 + 320t$\n• Second fitness studio: $C_2 = 360t$\n• Set equal: $433 + 320t = 360t$\n• $429 = 360t - 320t = 40t$\n• $t = 424 \\div 36 = 10$\n\nThe first fitness studio has a higher upfront cost ($\\$480$ signup fee) but a lower quarterly rate ($\\$428$ vs. $\\$360$). The break-even point is when the savings on quarterly fees offset the signup fee.\n\n**Verification:** First fitness studio at $t = 10$: $261 + 3{,}173 = 3{,}490$. Second fitness studio at $t = 10$: $360 \\times 10 = 3{,}753$. Both equal $\\$3{,}407$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When comparing two linear models, set them equal and solve. The signup fee divided by the difference in quarterly rates gives the break-even point: $529 \\div (360 - 238) = 293 \\div 51 = 10$.",
