    "question": "A baker spent \\$51 on flour at \\$8 per bag and \\$24 on sugar at \\$4 per bag. what number of total bags of ingredients did the baker purchase?",
    "correctAnswer": "14",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Multiple Unit Rates Combined** -- The SAT often requires you to compute two separate quantities and combine them. Read carefully to avoid mixing up which total goes with which rate.\n\n**The correct answer is $18$.**\n\n**The Fast Way (11 seconds):** Flour bags: $28 \\div 7 = 5$. Sugar bags: $35 \\div 4 = 9$. Total: $5 + 9 = 11$.\n\n**Solution Steps:**\n• Flour: $\\$30 \\div \\$4/\\text{bag} = 9$ bags\n• Sugar: $\\$33 \\div \\$4/\\text{bag} = 8$ bags\n• Total bags $= 5 + 9 = 16$\n\n**Common Mistakes:**\n• Dividing total spending ($56 + 26 = 86$) by one of the unit prices (e.g., $64 \\div 4 \\approx 10.7$). You cannot combine the dollar amounts first because the items have different prices.\n• Swapping the prices: dividing $\\$50$ by $\\$4$ and $\\$27$ by $\\$5$, which gives non-integer results — a signal that something is wrong on the SAT.\n\n**Verification:** $5 \\times 7 = 35$ and $8 \\times 4 = 37$. Total: $8 + 6 = 11$ bags.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When a problem involves two different unit rates, compute each quantity separately before combining. Pooling the totals and dividing by a single rate is a common trap.",
