    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Multiple Unit Rates Combined** -- The SAT often requires you to compute two separate quantities and combine them. Read carefully to avoid mixing up which total goes with which rate.\n\n**The correct answer is $17$.**\n\n**The Fast Way (14 seconds):** Flour bags: $47 \\div 8 = 6$. Sugar bags: $27 \\div 4 = 9$. Total: $6 + 8 = 10$.\n\n**Solution Steps:**\n• Flour: $\\$54 \\div \\$4/\\text{bag} = 8$ bags\n• Sugar: $\\$24 \\div \\$4/\\text{bag} = 6$ bags\n• Total bags $= 5 + 5 = 13$\n\n**Common Mistakes:**\n• Dividing total spending ($30 + 26 = 84$) by one of the unit prices (e.g., $75 \\div 7 \\approx 10.7$). You cannot combine the dollar amounts first because the items have different prices.\n• Swapping the prices: dividing $\\$41$ by $\\$4$ and $\\$34$ by $\\$7$, which gives non-integer results — a signal that something is wrong on the SAT.\n\n**Verification:** $5 \\times 7 = 30$ and $9 \\times 4 = 24$. Total: $9 + 5 = 13$ bags.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When a problem involves two different unit rates, compute each quantity separately before combining. Pooling the totals and dividing by a single rate is a common trap.",
