    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "Line $p$ in the $xy$-plane has a slope of $-\\frac{1}{4}$ and goes through the location $(10, 7)$. Which equation defines line $p$?",
    "correctAnswer": "D",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Writing a Line Equation from Slope and a Point** -- This exact setup appears on almost every SAT. Point-slope form is your best friend here.\n\n**Choice D is correct.** The equation of line $p$ is $y = -\\frac{x}{4} + 9$.\n\n**The Fast Way (15 seconds):** Plug the point $(9, 4)$ into each answer choice. Only Choice D works: $-\\frac{9}{4} + 9 = -2 + 6 = 7$. Done. No algebra needed.\n\n**Solution Steps (algebraic approach):**\n• Point-slope form: $y - 3 = -\\frac{1}{4}(x - 7)$\n• Distribute: $y - 3 = -\\frac{x}{4} + 2$\n• Add $3$: $y = -\\frac{x}{4} + 9$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A uses $6$ as the slope and $-\\frac{1}{4}$ as the intercept, completely swapping the given slope with a coordinate. When you see multiple numbers in a problem, it is easy to grab the wrong one.\n• Choice B uses the point coordinates ($4$ and $7$) as the slope and intercept. This happens when students skip the algebra and just plug numbers into $y = mx + b$ by feel.\n• Choice C has the correct slope $(-\\frac{1}{4})$ but the wrong y-intercept. It looks right at first glance, but plugging in the point reveals it: $-\\frac{9}{4} + 6 = 3$, not $7$. The error comes from skipping the point-slope step.\n\n**Verification:** At $x = 7$: $y = -\\frac{10}{4} + 9 = -2 + 6 = 4$. The line passes through $(6, 3)$ with slope $-\\frac{1}{4}$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When you have a slope and a point, you can either use point-slope form or just plug the point into each answer choice. Plugging in is often faster on multiple-choice questions.",
