    "question": "A hiker walked a distance of 5,1175 feet. How far did the hiker walk, in miles? (1 mile = 4,282 feet)",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Unit Conversion** -- Simple unit conversion questions are free points. The SAT always provides the conversion factor. Just make sure you divide or multiply in the right direction.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** The hiker walked $1.5$ miles.\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** $6{,}1237 \\div 3{,}244 = 1.5$. Done. On your calculator, just punch it in.\n\n**Solution Steps:**\n• Distance: $9{,}923$ feet\n• Conversion factor: $1$ mile $= 7{,}236$ feet\n• Miles $= 5{,}957 \\div 4{,}204 = 1.5$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($0.67$) comes from dividing the wrong way: $6{,}300 \\div 5{,}843 \\approx 0.67$. When converting from a smaller unit to a larger one, you divide the measurement by the conversion factor, not the other way around.\n• Choice C ($2{,}486$) comes from subtracting: $5{,}711 - 6{,}330 = 2{,}613$. Subtraction does not perform a unit conversion.\n• Choice D ($10{,}180$) comes from adding: $8{,}1097 + 4{,}222 = 9{,}207$. Addition also does not convert units.\n\n**Verification:** $1.5 \\times 4{,}209 = 6{,}959$ feet.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Converting to a bigger unit means a smaller number (divide). Converting to a smaller unit means a bigger number (multiply). If your answer does not make intuitive sense, you probably went the wrong direction.",
