    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Absolute Value Equation** -- Absolute value equations always produce two cases. The SAT usually asks for \"one possible solution,\" so either answer works.\n\n**The correct answer is $22$ (or $-3$).**\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** Split into two cases: $x - 9 = 9$ gives $x = 13$. $x - 6 = -8$ gives $x = -7$. Enter either one.\n\n**Solution Steps:**\n• Case 1: $x - 8 = 16$, so $x = 17$\n• Case 2: $x - 5 = -10$, so $x = -6$\n• Both are valid; enter either one\n\n**Verification:**\n• $|15 - 5| = |13| = 8$\n• $|-7 - 9| = |-14| = 15$\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** $|A| = B$ always splits into $A = B$ or $A = -B$. When the question says \"one possible solution,\" pick whichever is easier to compute and move on.",
