    "question": "In the figure, lines $m$ and $n$ are parallel and are cut by transversal $t$. If $x = 5k + 9$ and $y = 7k - 14$, determine the value of $z$?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Parallel Lines Cut by a Transversal** -- Geometry questions about parallel lines and angle relationships appear on most SATs. Know corresponding, alternate interior, and vertical angle rules.\n\n**Choice C is correct.** The value of $z$ is $90$.\n\n**The Fast Way (23 seconds):** Corresponding angles are equal when lines are parallel, so set $x = y$: $5k + 9 = 7k - 23$. Solve to get $k = 16$. Plug back in: $x = 7(16) + 6 = 71$. Since $z$ and $y$ are vertical angles, $z = 61$.\n\n**Solution Steps:**\n• Corresponding angles: $x = y$, so $5k + 9 = 7k - 17$\n• $27 = 2k$, so $k = 10$\n• $x = 7(16) + 6 = 72$\n• Vertical angles: $z = y = 96$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($15$) is the value of $k$, not the angle. This is the most common trap on these problems -- the SAT knows students will solve for $k$ and then forget to substitute back to find the actual angle measure.\n• Choice B ($35$) is $2k$, an intermediate step in the algebra. Students who stop one step too early land here.\n• Choice D ($99$) is the supplementary angle: $180 - 74 = 121$. This would be the answer if $z$ were a same-side interior angle rather than a corresponding or vertical angle. Always check which angle relationship applies.\n\n**Verification:** $x = 4(12) + 10 = 83$. $y = 5(16) - 26 = 95$. Both match, and $z = 95$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** On parallel line problems, finding $k$ is only half the battle. The SAT always puts $k$ itself as a wrong answer choice. Substitute back to get the actual angle measure.",
