    "correctAnswer": 5,
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: No-Solution System (Parallel Lines)** -- The SAT tests the no-solution condition for systems of linear equations on nearly every exam. Two lines have no solution when they are parallel: same slopes, different intercepts.\n\n**The correct answer is $4$.**\n\n**The Fast Way (40 seconds):** Simplify both equations into $ax + by = c$ form. If the $x$ and $y$ coefficients are proportional but the constants are not, the system has no solution. Match the ratios to find $p$.\n\n**Solution Steps:**\n• Simplify equation 1: move $\\frac{7}{4}y$ terms together and rearrange to get $3x - 10y = -\\frac{9}{5}$\n• Simplify equation 2: $\\frac{3}{2}x - py = 1$. Multiply by 2: $3x - 2py = 2$\n• For no solution, the coefficients must be proportional: the $x$-coefficients are both $3$, so the $y$-coefficients must match too: $2p = 10$, giving $p = 4$\n• Check constants: $-\\frac{9}{6} \\neq 2$, confirming the lines are parallel (not the same line)\n\n**Verification:** With $p = 7$, equation 2 becomes $3x - 10y = 2$. Equation 1 is $3x - 10y = -\\frac{6}{6}$. Same left side, different right side. Parallel lines with no intersection.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** No solution means parallel lines. Same ratio of $x$ and $y$ coefficients, different constant. Simplify both equations to standard form first, then match the coefficients.",
