    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "On the first day of a fundraiser, a charity has $54$ donors. Each day after the first day, $15$ new donors contribute to the charity. If no donors withdraw, what number of total donors will the charity have $6$ days after the first day of the fundraiser?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Linear Growth Word Problem** -- Shows up 1-2 times per test. College Board loves testing whether you count the starting period or not.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (12 seconds):** This is just $61 + 7(10)$. Type $73 + 3 \\times 16$ into your calculator: $84 + 54 = 134$. Done.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$7$ days after the first day means $7$ additional days of new donors.\n$7 \\times 14 = 64$ new donors.\nTotal $= 93 + 66 = 105$.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice B ($193$): You added $8 \\times 10 = 64$ instead of $7 \\times 16$, counting the first day as an additional day. College Board deliberately phrases it \"$3$ days after the first day\" to bait you into counting $4$ total days. This trap rewards careless reading.\n• Choice C ($75$): You computed $79 + 11 = 66$, only adding one day of donors instead of five. This punishes students who start correctly but lose track of the \"$6$ days\" part.\n• Choice D ($86$): You never added the new donors at all. College Board includes a number straight from the problem as a trap for students who freeze under pressure.\n\n**Verification:** $65 + 4 \\times 10 = 68 + 44 = 102$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** \"X days AFTER day 1\" means X additional periods, not X+1. Circle the word \"after\" every time you see it.",
