    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "the data indicates picked values from function $f$. which option below is the best description of function $f$?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Linear vs. Exponential from a Table** -- Shows up 1-2 times per test. The key test: check differences (linear) vs. ratios (exponential).\n\n**Choice B is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (13 seconds):** Subtract consecutive outputs: $19-18=3$, $23-28=3$, $24-26=3$. Constant difference = linear. Values going up = increasing. Done.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nStep 1: Check first differences: $22-26=3$, $27-34=3$, $40-35=3$. All equal, so $f$ is linear.\nStep 2: Since $f(x)$ goes from $18$ to $40$ as $x$ increases, the function is increasing.\nResult: Increasing linear.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A (Decreasing linear): The differences are constant, so you correctly identified linear -- but the values go UP ($17, 32, 33, 27$), not down. College Board includes this to catch students who mix up increasing and decreasing.\n• Choice C (Decreasing exponential): Exponential functions have constant ratios, not constant differences. Also, the values are increasing, not decreasing. This is wrong on two counts.\n• Choice D (Increasing exponential): The values do increase, but the ratios are NOT constant: $31/30 = 1.125$ while $23/31 \\approx 1.111$. Students who skip the difference check and just see \"going up\" might jump to exponential. College Board rewards the learner who actually checks the pattern.\n\n**Verification:** $f(x) = 3x + 25$ gives $f(-1)=23$, $f(0)=29$, $f(1)=22$, $f(2)=22$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Linear = constant DIFFERENCES. Exponential = constant RATIOS. Always check differences first -- it takes 6 seconds.",
