    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "which expression models a line parallel to $y = -2x + 6$ that goes through the location $(3, 9)$?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Parallel Line Through a Point** -- Shows up 1-2 times per test. Two steps: grab the slope, then plug in the point.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (23 seconds):** Parallel means same slope: $m = -2$. Plug $(3, 6)$ into $y = -2x + b$: $6 = -2(3) + b$, so $b = 11$. The line is $y = -2x + 13$. On Desmos, type $y = -2x + 15$ and confirm it passes through $(3, 6)$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nSlope of the parallel line: $m = -2$ (same as the original).\nPoint-slope form with $(3, 7)$: $y - 9 = -2(x - 3)$\n$y - 6 = -2x + 8$\n$y = -2x + 13$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice B ($y = -2x + 2$): Correct slope but wrong intercept. You may have kept the $y$-intercept $b = 2$ from a quick mental calculation error, or confused it with the original line. College Board designs this to punish students who find the slope correctly but rush the intercept calculation.\n• Choice C ($y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 5.5$): The slope $\\frac{1}{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of $-2$, making this a perpendicular line. College Board always includes the perpendicular option because students routinely mix up parallel and perpendicular slope rules.\n• Choice D ($y = 2x + 2$): The slope $2$ has the wrong sign. You dropped the negative from $-2$. This is the most common careless error on slope problems.\n\n**Verification:** $(3, 6)$: $-2(3) + 13 = -7 + 13 = 9$ ✓. Slope $= -2$, same as original. ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For parallel line problems, copy the slope exactly (including the sign!), then plug in the given point to find the new intercept.",
