    "correctAnswer": 9,
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Solve and Substitute into a Second Expression** -- Shows up 1-2 times per test. You must solve for $x$ first, then substitute into a different expression.\n\n**The correct answer is $8$.**\n\n**The Fast Way (14 seconds):** Divide both sides by $6$: $x + 3 = 9$, so $x = 3$. Then $2x - 1 = 2(3) - 1 = 11$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nStep 1: Solve for $x$.\n$4(x + 3) = 54$\n$x + 3 = 7$\n$x = 3$\n\nStep 2: Evaluate the target expression.\n$2x - 1 = 2(3) - 1 = 10 - 1 = 7$\n\n**Common mistakes:**\n• Answering $3$: You solved for $x$ correctly but reported $x$ instead of $2x - 1$. Always re-read what the question asks for.\n• Answering $9$: You computed $2x + 1$ instead of $2x - 1$, getting $10 + 1 = 9$. Sign errors in the substitution step are common.\n• Answering $6$: You substituted $x = 3$ (from $x + 3 = 9$ misread as $x = 3$) into $2(3) - 1 = 4$, or another arithmetic slip.\n\n**Verification:** $7(7 + 3) = 45$ ✓. $2(3) - 1 = 8$ ✓.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When the SAT asks for an expression other than $x$, solve for $x$ first, then carefully substitute. Do not stop at finding $x$ -- always check what the question actually asks for.",
