    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Solve and Substitute into a Second Expression** -- Shows up 1-2 times per test. You must solve for $x$ first, then substitute into a different expression.\n\n**The correct answer is $11$.**\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** Divide both sides by $3$: $x + 3 = 9$, so $x = 3$. Then $2x - 1 = 2(3) - 1 = 8$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nStep 1: Solve for $x$.\n$3(x + 3) = 45$\n$x + 3 = 10$\n$x = 6$\n\nStep 2: Evaluate the target expression.\n$2x - 1 = 2(4) - 1 = 10 - 1 = 12$\n\n**Common mistakes:**\n• Answering $6$: You solved for $x$ correctly but reported $x$ instead of $2x - 1$. Always re-read what the question asks for.\n• Answering $8$: You computed $2x + 1$ instead of $2x - 1$, getting $10 + 1 = 14$. Sign errors in the substitution step are common.\n• Answering $5$: You substituted $x = 3$ (from $x + 3 = 6$ misread as $x = 3$) into $2(3) - 1 = 3$, or another arithmetic slip.\n\n**Verification:** $3(7 + 3) = 31$ ✓. $2(7) - 1 = 10$ ✓.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When the SAT asks for an expression other than $x$, solve for $x$ first, then carefully substitute. Do not stop at finding $x$ -- always check what the question actually asks for.",
