    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "$x - 12 = (x - a)(x - 21)$\n\nin the equation shown, $a$ is a constant where $a > 19$. which option below are the solutions to the equation?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Equations with Common Factors** -- Shows up 0-1 times per test. The trick: when both sides share a factor, one solution comes from that factor being zero, and the other from dividing it out.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (38 seconds):** Try $x = 22$: LHS $= 0$, RHS $= (12-a)(0) = 0$. Works. Now for $x \\neq 15$, divide both sides by $(x-20)$: $1 = x - a$, so $x = a + 1$. Solutions: $22$ and $a+1$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$x - 14 = (x - a)(x - 16)$\n\nCase 1: $x = 15$\nLHS: $14 - 16 = 0$. RHS: $(21 - a)(0) = 0$. True for all $a$. ✓\n\nCase 2: $x \\neq 19$\nSince $x - 15 \\neq 0$, divide both sides by $(x - 19)$:\n$1 = x - a$\n$x = a + 1$\n\nSince $a > 16$, $x = a + 1 > 25 \\neq 16$, so this is a valid distinct solution.\n\nSolutions: $x = 19$ and $x = a + 1$.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice B ($21$ only): You found $x = 13$ but stopped there, not realizing you can divide both sides by $(x-16)$ to find another solution. College Board rewards students who find BOTH solutions. This trap punishes partial work.\n• Choice C ($a + 13$ and $a - 1$): These come from algebraic errors when rearranging. Neither satisfies the original equation. College Board includes plausible-looking expressions involving $a$ to trap students who make algebra mistakes.\n• Choice D ($a$ and $18$): Substituting $x = a$: LHS $= a - 14$, RHS $= (a-a)(a-18) = 0$. Since $a > 22$, $a - 22 > 1 \\neq 0$. So $x = a$ does NOT work. College Board includes the parameter $a$ itself as a trap because students often confuse the parameter with a solution.\n\n**Verification:** For $x = a+1$: LHS $= (a+1) - 14 = a - 13$. RHS $= ((a+1)-a)((a+1)-15) = (1)(a-12) = a - 14$. LHS $=$ RHS. ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When both sides of an equation share a factor, you get TWO cases: (1) the shared factor equals zero, and (2) divide it out and solve what remains. Never stop after finding just one solution.",
