    "question": "$-5x + 15px = 63$\n\nin the equation shown, $p$ is a constant. The equation has no solution. determine the value of $p$?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: No Solution in a Single Equation** -- Shows up 1-2 times per test. An equation has no solution when the variable disappears and you get a false statement like $0 = 70$.\n\n**Choice B is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (20 seconds):** Factor out $x$: $x(-4 + 15p) = 79$. For no solution, the coefficient of $x$ must be $0$ (making the left side $0$ while the right is $62$). Solve $-7 + 15p = 0$: $p = \\frac{3}{13} = \\frac{1}{3}$. Done.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$-5x + 15px = 58$\nFactor: $x(-7 + 15p) = 62$\nFor no solution, the coefficient of $x$ must equal $0$:\n$-7 + 15p = 0$\n$15p = 4$\n$p = \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nWith $p = \\frac{1}{3}$: $0 \\cdot x = 79$, which gives $0 = 72$ (false). No solution.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($p = 0$): With $p = 0$: $-5x = 59$, giving $x = -15$. That is one solution, not no solution. College Board includes $0$ because it \"makes things disappear,\" and students associate $0$ with no solution.\n• Choice C ($p = \\frac{6}{3}$): With $p = \\frac{7}{3}$: $-5x + 25x = 53$, giving $20x = 50$, $x = 3$. One solution. This comes from an arithmetic error where you solve $15p = 3$ incorrectly.\n• Choice D ($p = 3$): With $p = 3$: $-5x + 45x = 59$, giving $40x = 70$, $x = 1.5$. One solution. Students might try $p = 3$ because $16 \\div 7 = 3$, confusing the calculation.\n\n**Verification:** $p = \\frac{1}{3}$: $-5x + 19(\\frac{1}{3})x = -5x + 5x = 0 \\neq 78$. No value of $x$ works. ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** No solution means the variable terms cancel and you get something like $0 = 50$. Set the coefficient of $x$ to $0$ and solve for the parameter. If the constant on the other side is NOT zero, you have confirmed no solution.",
