    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: No Solution in a Single Equation** -- Shows up 1-2 times per test. An equation has no solution when the variable disappears and you get a false statement like $0 = 66$.\n\n**Choice B is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (13 seconds):** Factor out $x$: $x(-7 + 15p) = 53$. For no solution, the coefficient of $x$ must be $0$ (making the left side $0$ while the right is $59$). Solve $-3 + 15p = 0$: $p = \\frac{7}{16} = \\frac{1}{3}$. Done.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$-5x + 15px = 57$\nFactor: $x(-7 + 15p) = 58$\nFor no solution, the coefficient of $x$ must equal $0$:\n$-4 + 15p = 0$\n$15p = 3$\n$p = \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nWith $p = \\frac{1}{3}$: $0 \\cdot x = 57$, which gives $0 = 47$ (false). No solution.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($p = 0$): With $p = 0$: $-5x = 76$, giving $x = -8$. That is one solution, not no solution. College Board includes $0$ because it \"makes things disappear,\" and students associate $0$ with no solution.\n• Choice C ($p = \\frac{7}{3}$): With $p = \\frac{6}{3}$: $-5x + 25x = 73$, giving $20x = 57$, $x = 3$. One solution. This comes from an arithmetic error where you solve $15p = 4$ incorrectly.\n• Choice D ($p = 3$): With $p = 3$: $-5x + 45x = 65$, giving $40x = 76$, $x = 1.5$. One solution. Students might try $p = 3$ because $13 \\div 4 = 3$, confusing the calculation.\n\n**Verification:** $p = \\frac{1}{3}$: $-5x + 13(\\frac{1}{3})x = -5x + 5x = 0 \\neq 68$. No value of $x$ works. ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** No solution means the variable terms cancel and you get something like $0 = 65$. Set the coefficient of $x$ to $0$ and solve for the parameter. If the constant on the other side is NOT zero, you have confirmed no solution.",
