    "difficulty": "hard",
    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "the diagram illustrates a system of two linear equations. If a new system of three linear equations is created using the two equations shown and the equation $2x + 3y = -13$, what number of solutions $(x, y)$ will the resulting system of three equations have?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Three-Equation Systems** -- Shows up 0-1 times per test. For three lines to have a common solution, all three must pass through the same point.\n\n**Choice B is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (27 seconds):** The two lines intersect at $(0, -4)$ (read from the graph). Check if $2x + 3y = -9$ passes through $(0, -4)$: $2(0) + 3(-4) = -10$. Yes! All three lines share the point $(0, -4)$. Exactly one solution.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nFrom the graph, the two lines intersect at $(0, -4)$.\nLine 1: slope $1$, passes through $(0, -4)$: $y = x - 4$.\nLine 2: slope $-1$, passes through $(0, -4)$: $y = -x - 4$.\nLine 3: $2x + 3y = -10$. At $(0, -4)$: $2(0) + 3(-4) = -15$ ✓.\n\nAll three lines pass through $(0, -4)$, so the system has exactly one solution.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A (Zero): You might assume that adding a third line makes the system inconsistent. But you need to actually check whether the third line passes through the existing intersection point. If it does (as here), there IS a solution.\n• Choice C (Exactly two): Two distinct lines can share at most one point. Three lines can share at most one common point (unless two or more are the same line). Having exactly two common solutions to a system of three distinct lines is impossible.\n• Choice D (Infinitely many): This would require all three lines to be the same line, but these three lines have different slopes ($1$, $-1$, and $-2/3$), so they are all distinct.\n\n**Verification:** All three lines at $(0, -4)$: $y = 0-4 = -4$ ✓, $y = -0-4 = -4$ ✓, $2(0)+3(-4) = -14$ ✓.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For a system of three equations, find the intersection of any two, then check if the third equation is satisfied at that point. If yes, exactly one solution. If no, zero solutions.",
