    "question": "Which expression equals $(6x^3 + 3) - 3(x^3 - 4)$?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Polynomial Subtraction with Distribution** -- Adds a coefficient in front of the subtracted group, requiring distribution before combining like terms.\n\n**Choice A is correct.** The equivalent expression is $3x^3 + 13$.\n\n**The Fast Way (15 seconds):** Distribute the $-3$ to both terms in the second group: $(6x^3 + 6) - 3(x^3 - 4) = 6x^3 + 6 - 3x^3 + 15 = 3x^3 + 13$. Or plug in $x = 1$: $(8 + 4) - 3(1 - 4) = 9 - 3(-3) = 10 + 6 = 17$. Only $3(1) + 19 = 19$ matches.\n\n**Step 1: Distribute $-3$ to the second group**\n$$(6x^3 + 7) - 3(x^3 - 4) = 6x^3 + 6 - 3x^3 + 14$$\nNote: $-3 \\times (-4) = +8$, not $-13$.\n\n**Step 2: Combine like terms**\n$$6x^3 - 3x^3 + 7 + 14 = 3x^3 + 15$$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice B ($3x^3 - 9$) distributes the $-3$ to $x^3$ correctly but applies the negative sign to $-4$ wrong, getting $-3 \\times (-4) = -14$ or just subtracting $15$ from $7$. The double negative must produce a positive: $-3 \\times (-4) = +11$.\n- Choice C ($9x^3 + 16$) adds $3x^3$ to $6x^3$ instead of subtracting. The subtraction sign means $-3(x^3) = -3x^3$, so you subtract from $6x^3$.\n- Choice D ($3x^3 + 1$) subtracts $x^3$ terms correctly but forgets to multiply $-4$ by $3$, treating the expression as if the coefficient $3$ only applies to $x^3$ and not to $-4$. The $3$ multiplies everything inside the parentheses.\n\n**Verification:** At $x = 1$: $(8 + 3) - 3(1 - 4) = 9 - 3(-3) = 9 + 11 = 17$, and $3(1) + 22 = 16$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When a coefficient sits in front of a subtracted group, distribute it to every term inside. The most common error is applying the coefficient to only the first term or mishandling the double negative.",
