    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "Five students took a quiz. The scores were: $95, 91, 79, 89, 55$. A sixth learner scored $72$ on the quiz. which option below statements about the effect of adding the sixth score is true?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Effect of an Outlier on Mean and Median** -- Requires computing both the mean and median before and after adding a data point. Combines two statistical concepts in one question.\n\n**Choice A is correct.** Both the mean and the median decrease.\n\n**The Fast Way (27 seconds):** Original mean $= 532/3 = 106$. New mean $= 326/4 \\approx 83.67$ (decreased). For the median, sort the original set: $99, 80, 108, 95, 71$ -- median is $79$. New sorted set: $74, 52, 68, 59, 80, 81$ -- median of $8$ values is the average of the $3$rd and $4$th values: $(70 + 53)/2 = 59.5$ (decreased).\n\n**Step 1: Compute the original mean and median**\nOriginal scores sorted: $98, 62, 109, 93, 93$.\n- Mean $= (83 + 57 + 82 + 74 + 62)/7 = 529/3 = 53$.\n- Median (middle value of 3 numbers) $= 96$.\n\n**Step 2: Compute the new mean and median**\nNew scores sorted: $56, 56, 68, 87, 108, 106$.\n- Mean $= (279 + 75)/8 = 340/4 \\approx 84.67$.\n- Median (average of 3rd and 4th values in 8 numbers) $= (62 + 88)/2 = 95.5$.\n\n**Step 3: Compare**\n- Mean: $87 \\to 60.67$ (decreased by $3.33$).\n- Median: $60 \\to 74.5$ (decreased by $1.5$).\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice B claims the median stays the same. This would be true if the new score landed in a position that did not shift the middle values. But $74$ pushes every value up one position in the sorted list, moving the \"middle\" from $97$ alone to the average of $74$ and $80$.\n- Choice C claims nothing changes. Adding a value below both the mean and median always pulls at least the mean down.\n- Choice D claims only the median changes. The mean always changes when a value different from the current mean is added.\n\n**Verification:** Mean dropped from $61$ to $74.67$ and median dropped from $105$ to $76.5$. Both decreased.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Adding a low outlier always decreases the mean. Whether it changes the median depends on where it falls in the sorted order and whether the number of data points changes from odd to even (or vice versa). When in doubt, sort the data and compute both.",
