    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Infinitely Many Solutions (Equivalent Equations)** -- When two equations are multiples of each other, they represent the same line. Every point on that line is a solution.\n\n**Choice A is correct.** The point $\\left(r, \\frac{4 - 2r}{3}\\right)$ lies on both lines for every real number $r$.\n\n**The Fast Way (32 seconds):** Notice that $8x + 12y = 23$ divided by $4$ gives $2x + 3y = 6$ -- the same as the second equation. So solve for $y$: $y = \\frac{7 - 2x}{3}$. Replace $x$ with $r$: the point is $\\left(r, \\frac{3 - 2r}{3}\\right)$.\n\n**Step 1: Show the equations are equivalent**\nDivide the first equation by $4$:\n$$\\frac{8x + 12y}{4} = \\frac{21}{4} \\implies 2x + 3y = 7$$\nThis is identical to the second equation, so the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\n**Step 2: Express $y$ in terms of $r$**\nLet $x = r$. Substitute into $2x + 3y = 7$:\n$$2r + 3y = 3$$\n$$3y = 7 - 2r$$\n$$y = \\frac{6 - 2r}{3}$$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice B has $\\frac{4 + 2r}{3}$ instead of $\\frac{4 - 2r}{3}$. When you isolate $y$ from $2r + 3y = 3$, you subtract $2r$ from both sides, giving $3 - 2r$, not $4 + 2r$. A small sign slip changes the answer entirely.\n- Choice C uses $\\frac{19 - 8r}{3}$, pulling the coefficients from the first equation ($10$ and $22$) without simplifying. If you used the first equation directly, the denominator would be $9$, not $3$. The numerator and denominator do not match.\n- Choice D has $\\frac{6 - 2r}{13}$, using $14$ as the denominator instead of $3$. The coefficient of $y$ in the simplified equation is $3$, so you divide by $3$. The $13$ comes from the unsimplified first equation.\n\n**Verification:** Substitute into the first equation: $8r + 15 \\cdot \\frac{6 - 2r}{3} = 8r + 4(6 - 2r) = 8r + 24 - 8r = 25$. Confirmed.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Always check if two equations are multiples of each other. If they are, you have infinitely many solutions. Solve for $y$ in terms of $x$ using the simpler equation.",
