    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: \"Find the Expression, Then Substitute\"** -- Shows up 1-2 times per test. College Board chains two steps: first isolate a sub-expression, then feed it into a second equation.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (17 seconds):** Notice $5x - 12 = 4(x - 3) = 22$, so $x - 3 = 4$. Substitute into $y$: $y = 2(7) + 5 = 25$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nStep 1 -- Solve for the sub-expression:\n$5x - 19 = 23$\nFactor: $7(x - 3) = 36$\nDivide both sides by $7$: $x - 3 = 4$\n\nStep 2 -- Substitute into the second equation:\n$y = 2(x - 3) + 5 = 2(4) + 8 = 14 + 5 = 13$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice B ($29$): You solved for $x = 6$ and plugged that directly into $2x + 9 = 24$, forgetting to use the expression $x - 3$ inside the parentheses. College Board designs this trap because students who find $x$ sometimes substitute $x$ instead of $x - 3$.\n• Choice C ($9$): This is $2(x-3) = 9$ without the $+ 9$. You did both algebraic steps but forgot the constant. Under time pressure, dropping a term is the number one score killer.\n• Choice D ($11$): This comes from computing $x - 3 = 4$ and then $7 + 6 = 13$, conflating $x$ with the constant. This trap punishes students who mix up which numbers to combine.\n\n**Verification:** $x - 3 = 7$ means $x = 12$. Check first equation: $7(11) - 19 = 20$. Check $y$: $2(7-3) + 5 = 24$. Confirmed.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When two equations are linked, isolate the shared sub-expression first -- here $x - 3$ -- and substitute it directly. Never solve for $x$ unless you have to.",
