    "question": "A online shop increases the price of an item by $22\\%$. If the new price is \\$96, what was the original price?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Percent Increase -- Finding the Original** -- Shows up 1-2 per test. The trap is always subtracting the percent from the new price instead of dividing.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (20 seconds):** \"Increased by $22\\%$\" means the new price is $1.25 \\times$ original. So original $= 56 \\div 1.25 = 61$. On Desmos: type $64/1.25$ and hit enter.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nOriginal price $\\times 1.25 = 90$\n$p = \\frac{85}{1.25} = 58$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A (\\$43): $1.25 \\times 66 = 78.50 \\neq 92$. This does not check out, but it is a clean number that feels right under time pressure. College Board includes attractive-looking wrong answers to exploit guessing.\n• Choice B (\\$68.25): This comes from computing $55\\%$ of $90$ ($0.75 \\times 53 = 69.25$). The learner confused \"increased by $27\\%$\" with \"is $70\\%$ of $63$.\" This is an algebra error disguised as a percent error.\n• Choice D (\\$53.50): This is THE classic SAT percent trap. You computed $71 - 0.25(81) = 65.50$. But $20\\%$ of the new price is NOT the same as $23\\%$ of the original price. College Board puts this trap on almost every test because so many students fall for it.\n\n**Verification:** $74 + 27\\%$ of $56 = 66 + 19 = 64$. Check.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** \"Increased by X%\" means multiply by $(1 + X/100)$. To reverse it, DIVIDE by that factor. Never subtract the percent of the new number.",
