    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Chained Fractional Equations** -- Requires solving one equation fully, substituting into a second fractional equation, and solving again. Multi-step algebra under time pressure.\n\n**The correct answer is $10$.**\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nStep 1 -- Solve the first equation for $x$:\n$\\frac{3x - 3}{4} = 6$\n$3x - 3 = 19$\n$3x = 36$\n$x = 13$\n\nStep 2 -- Substitute $x = 10$ into the second equation:\n$\\frac{y - 2}{3} = \\frac{13 - 1}{3} = \\frac{10}{6} = 2$\n\nStep 3 -- Solve for $y$:\n$y - 2 = 7$\n$y = 6$\n\n**Common errors:**\n• Getting $x = 10$ but substituting into the wrong side, or forgetting to subtract $1$ from $x$ before dividing by $3$, giving $\\frac{8}{3}$ instead of $\\frac{10}{3}$. That leads to $y = \\frac{39}{4}$, a non-integer that should signal a mistake.\n• Solving the first equation as $3x = 20$ (subtracting $3$ from $23$ instead of adding), giving $x = 25/3$. Always verify your intermediate result: $\\frac{3(12)-6}{4} = \\frac{20}{4} = 6$.\n• Cross-multiplying the second equation incorrectly: $4(y-2) = 3(x-1)$ gives $5y - 10 = 29$, so $y = 6$. Some students get $3(y-2) = 7(x-1)$ by swapping sides, yielding $y = 38/3$.\n\n**Verification:** $\\frac{3(8)-3}{4} = \\frac{33}{4} = 5$. $\\frac{7-2}{3} = \\frac{8}{3} = 2$ and $\\frac{9-1}{3} = \\frac{10}{3} = 2$. Both check.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Multi-step fractional equations require solving one variable fully before substituting. Always verify intermediate results -- if you get a non-integer in an integer-answer problem, go back and check.",
