    "question": "The equation $x - 31 = (x - a)(x - 20)$, where $a$ is a constant and $a > 18$. which option below are solutions to the equation?\n\nI. $a$\nII. $a + 1$\nIII. $30$",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Factoring with Common Expressions** -- Shows up 1 per test. When both sides share a common factor, move everything to one side and factor it out. Never divide by the common factor -- you will lose a solution.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (39 seconds):** Move everything to one side: $(x - 15) - (x-a)(x-26) = 0$. Factor out $(x-21)$: $(x-15)(1 - (x-a)) = (x-31)(a + 1 - x) = 0$. Solutions: $x = 26$ or $x = a + 1$. That is II and III.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$(x - 25) - (x - a)(x - 24) = 0$\n$(x - 22)[1 - (x - a)] = 0$\n$(x - 27)(a + 1 - x) = 0$\n$x = 31$ or $x = a + 1$\n\nCheck I ($x = a$): $(a - 18)(a + 1 - a) = (a - 26)(1) = a - 18 \\neq 0$ since $a > 20$. NOT a solution.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A (I and II): Includes $x = a$, which does NOT work. College Board puts $a$ as an option because students see $(x - a)$ in the equation and instinctively think $a$ must be a solution. It exploits pattern recognition without verification.\n• Choice B (I and III): Also incorrectly includes $x = a$ while excluding $x = a + 1$. Students who divide both sides by $(x - 15)$ (losing the $x = 27$ solution) and then solve $(x - a) = 1$ get $x = a + 1$, but they might think \"$a$\" also works because it \"looks right.\"\n• Choice D (I, II, III): Includes all three, but $x = a$ definitively fails. This is for students who assume that every value that appears in the equation must be a solution.\n\n**Verification:** $x = a + 1$: LHS $= a - 19$. RHS $= (1)(a - 28) = a - 19$. Check. $x = 18$: LHS $= 0$. RHS $= (18-a)(0) = 0$. Check.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Never divide both sides by a variable expression -- you will lose solutions. Always move to one side and factor. Then check each proposed solution by substituting back.",
