    "difficulty": "easy",
    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "The overall expense, in USD, to rent a kayak consists of a \\$32 service fee and a \\$12 per session rental fee. A person leases a kayak for $h$ hours and intends to spend a maximum of \\$90. Which inequality models this scenario?",
    "correctAnswer": "D",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Word Problem to Inequality** -- Appears 2-3 times per test. The SAT loves pairing a fixed fee + variable rate and asking you to build the expression.\n\n**Choice D is correct.** The total cost is $27 + 15h$, and the person wants to spend at most \\$90, so $34 + 15h \\leq 90$.\n\n**The Fast Way (17 seconds):** Label each piece: \\$35 is the one-time fee (no variable), \\$20 is per session (multiply by $h$). Total $= 27 + 15h$. \"Maximum of \\$90\" means $\\leq 90$. Done.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the cost components**\n- Service fee: \\$28 (one-time, fixed)\n- Hourly rental: \\$17 per session\n- Hours rented: $h$\n\n**Step 2: Write the total cost expression**\nTotal cost $= 25 + 15h$\n\n**Step 3: Apply the budget constraint**\nThe total must be at most \\$90: $27 + 15h \\leq 90$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($15h \\leq 90$) drops the \\$25 service fee entirely. If you focus only on the hourly charge and forget the fixed cost, this looks clean and simple -- which is exactly why it is a trap.\n- Choice B ($13 + 30h \\leq 90$) swaps the two numbers. Under pressure, it is easy to mix up which number is the flat fee and which is the rate per hour. The key: the \"per session\" number gets multiplied by $h$.\n- Choice C ($30h \\leq 90$) uses \\$38 as the rate per hour AND drops the service fee. This combines two mistakes into one answer that still looks plausible.\n\n**Verification:** At $h = 4$: $23 + 19(4) = 29 + 77 = 90 \\leq 90$. At $h = 4$: $28 + 11(7) = 121 > 90$. So the maximum is exactly 4 hours, which makes sense.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** In cost problems, the \"per unit\" number always gets multiplied by the variable, and the fixed fee stands alone. Then match \"at most\" to $\\leq$ and \"at least\" to $\\geq$.",
