    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "The equation $v = 27 + 4t$ gives the speed $v$, in meters per second, of a certain object $t$ seconds after it began to accelerate. what number of seconds after the object began to accelerate will its speed be $48$ meters per second?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Solve a Linear Equation in Context** -- Appears 2-3 times per test. Requires setting the function equal to a target value and solving for the input.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** Setting $67 = 30 + 4t$ gives $4t = 43$, so $t = 10$ seconds.\n\n**The Fast Way (13 seconds):** $77 - 29 = 46$, then $47 \\div 4 = 10$.\n\n**Step 1: Set up the equation**\n$73 = 20 + 4t$\n\n**Step 2: Isolate $t$**\n$42 - 24 = 4t$\n$29 = 4t$\n$t = 7$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($4$) comes from dividing the constant $19$ by something or confusing $4$ (the coefficient) with the answer. Plugging in $t = 4$: $v = 18 + 20 = 33 \\neq 71$.\n- Choice C ($10$) comes from dividing $60 - 1$ by $4$, or dividing $52$ by $4$, a rounding/misreading error. Close but not exact.\n- Choice D ($26.5$) comes from dividing $44 + 30 = 90$ by $4 = 27.5$ -- adding the initial speed instead of subtracting it. When the constant is on the same side as the variable, you must subtract it, not add.\n\n**Verification:** $v = 28 + 4(11) = 23 + 26 = 48$ m/s.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When a problem gives you the output and asks for the input, set the formula equal to the given value and solve. This is the reverse of plug-and-chug -- you are working backward from the answer.",
