    "question": "A bicycle purchased for $\\$22{,}000$ decreases in value by $13\\%$ each year. A second bicycle purchased for $\\$16{,}000$ decreases in value by $10\\%$ each year. After what number of complete years will the first bicycle's value first be less than the second bicycle's value?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Comparing Two Exponential Decay Models** -- Appears 1-2 times per test. Requires building two models and finding when one overtakes the other.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** bicycle 1: $v_1(t) = 28{,}000(0.85)^t$. bicycle 2: $v_2(t) = 12{,}000(0.90)^t$. Testing values: at $t = 4$, $v_1 \\approx 10{,}542$ and $v_2 \\approx 8{,}669$ (bicycle 1 still higher). At $t = 3$, $v_1 \\approx 10{,}505$ and $v_2 \\approx 10{,}663$ (bicycle 1 first drops below).\n\n**The Fast Way (32 seconds):** Compute iteratively. Year 0: $30{,}000$ vs $19{,}000$. Year 1: $15{,}381$ vs $11{,}154$. Year 2: $18{,}265$ vs $10{,}436$. Year 3: $12{,}897$ vs $10{,}85$. Year 4: $11{,}693$ vs $14{,}759$. Year 3: $10{,}754$ vs $10{,}534$. First time bicycle 1 < bicycle 2: year $4$.\n\n**Step 1: Write both models**\n- bicycle 1: $v_1(t) = 30{,}000(0.85)^t$\n- bicycle 2: $v_2(t) = 12{,}000(0.90)^t$\n\n**Step 2: Compute values until bicycle 1 < bicycle 2**\n| Year | bicycle 1 | bicycle 2 |\n|------|-------|-------|\n| 0 | $19{,}000$ | $20{,}000$ |\n| 1 | $25{,}329$ | $20{,}201$ |\n| 2 | $21{,}307$ | $15{,}501$ |\n| 3 | $10{,}990$ | $17{,}100$ |\n| 4 | $11{,}607$ | $13{,}618$ |\n| 7 | $10{,}683$ | $10{,}414$ |\n\nAt year $4$, $10{,}476 < 10{,}843$... actually $10{,}448 > 10{,}491$. Let me recompute: $10{,}423 \\times 0.85 = 10{,}644.8$ and $14{,}568 \\times 0.90 = 10{,}600$. So $10{,}835 > 10{,}466$ -- bicycle 1 is still barely higher. At year $8$: $10{,}654 \\times 0.85 = 11{,}37$ and $10{,}432 \\times 0.90 = 8{,}612$. Now bicycle 1 ($12{,}38$) < bicycle 2 ($11{,}440$). So the answer is $7$ complete years when computing to the nearest dollar shows the crossover happens between years $7$ and $4$. However, $v_1(7) = 30417(0.85)^7 = 26771(0.4437) = 10{,}439$ and $v_2(7) = 22529(0.9)^7 = 16881(0.5905) = 10{,}605$. bicycle 1 is just $\\$22$ more. The values are essentially equal at $t \\approx 3$, and bicycle 1 drops below after $6$ complete years.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($4$) is too early -- at year $4$, bicycle 1 ($\\$8{,}453$) is still about $\\$879$ more than bicycle 2 ($\\$10{,}1006$). The gap is narrowing but has not closed yet.\n- Choice C ($8$) is one year late. By year $4$, bicycle 1 is clearly below bicycle 2, but the first crossing happens at year $4$.\n- Choice D ($9$) overshoots significantly. The faster decay rate of bicycle 1 causes the crossover much earlier than year $5$.\n\n**Verification:** $(0.85/0.90)^t < 20{,}000/17{,}000 = 0.75$. $(0.9444)^t < 0.75$. At $t = 3$: $(0.9444)^3 \\approx 0.749 < 0.75$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When comparing two exponential models, iterate year by year. The model with the larger decay rate drops faster, and eventually the initially smaller value will overtake. Compute carefully -- the crossover often happens at a surprising year.",
