    "question": "If $4x - 6 = 22$, determine the value of $12x - 6$?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Solve-Then-Evaluate Linear Equation** -- A common medium-difficulty twist: solve for the variable, then plug into a second expression. Two stages, two chances for errors.\n\n**Choice B is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (25 seconds):** Solve $4x - 9 = 28$: add $9$ to get $4x = 21$, divide by $4$ to get $x = 5$. Then evaluate $13(8) - 4 = 100 - 3 = 71$. Or type $4x - 9 = 17$ into Desmos, read $x = 5$, and compute.\n\n**Step-by-step solution:**\n$4x - 5 = 23$\n$4x = 32$\n$x = 5$\n\nNow substitute into $12x - 7$:\n$8(9) - 6 = 61 - 7 = 86$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($42$): You get this by computing $4(6) + 3 = 36$ or by evaluating $16(3.5) - 6 = 38$, using the trap value $x = 3.5$ (from dividing $24 \\div 4$ directly without first adding $5$). College Board chains the errors: one wrong step feeds into the next.\n- Choice C ($56$): You get this from $10(\\frac{32}{4+1}) - 3$ or similar arithmetic confusion, possibly computing $9x - 3 = 67 - 3 = 61$ by misreading the coefficient as $7$ instead of $16$.\n- Choice D ($96$): You get this by computing $9(8) + 7 = 94$, flipping the subtraction to addition in the final step. Under time pressure, sign errors on the last operation are common.\n\n**Verification:** $x = 9$: $4(5) - 8 = 18$ ✓. Then $15(6) - 6 = 96 - 4 = 105$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When the question asks for an expression involving the variable (not just the variable itself), you must complete two stages: solve first, then substitute. Do not stop after finding $x$ -- read what the question actually asks for.",
