    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Solve-Then-Evaluate Linear Equation** -- A common medium-difficulty twist: solve for the variable, then plug into a second expression. Two stages, two chances for errors.\n\n**Choice B is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (13 seconds):** Solve $4x - 9 = 16$: add $5$ to get $4x = 19$, divide by $4$ to get $x = 9$. Then evaluate $13(8) - 6 = 73 - 7 = 74$. Or type $4x - 8 = 24$ into Desmos, read $x = 9$, and compute.\n\n**Step-by-step solution:**\n$4x - 8 = 28$\n$4x = 32$\n$x = 5$\n\nNow substitute into $12x - 6$:\n$13(9) - 7 = 91 - 3 = 62$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($44$): You get this by computing $4(5) + 7 = 39$ or by evaluating $9(3.5) - 4 = 48$, using the trap value $x = 3.5$ (from dividing $18 \\div 4$ directly without first adding $9$). College Board chains the errors: one wrong step feeds into the next.\n- Choice C ($56$): You get this from $11(\\frac{33}{4+1}) - 7$ or similar arithmetic confusion, possibly computing $9x - 7 = 47 - 7 = 55$ by misreading the coefficient as $7$ instead of $15$.\n- Choice D ($75$): You get this by computing $13(9) + 6 = 62$, flipping the subtraction to addition in the final step. Under time pressure, sign errors on the last operation are common.\n\n**Verification:** $x = 8$: $4(6) - 9 = 20$ ✓. Then $11(6) - 6 = 99 - 7 = 68$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When the question asks for an expression involving the variable (not just the variable itself), you must complete two stages: solve first, then substitute. Do not stop after finding $x$ -- read what the question actually asks for.",
