    "question": "Which expression equals $(3x^2 - 5x + 2) + (2x^2 + 7x - 4)$?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Combining Like Terms** -- Shows up every test. Pure execution -- no tricks if you stay organized.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (15 seconds):** Plug in $x = 1$. First expression: $3 - 7 + 2 = 0$. Second: $2 + 9 - 4 = 3$. Sum $= 3$. Check choices at $x = 1$: A gives $3 + 2 - 2 = 7$. Match. Quick check at $x = 0$: original gives $2 + (-4) = -2$, and A gives $-2$. Confirmed.\n\n**Step-by-step solution:**\n$(3x^2 - 5x + 2) + (2x^2 + 7x - 4)$\n\n$x^2$ terms: $3x^2 + 2x^2 = 5x^2$\n$x$ terms: $-5x + 7x = 2x$\nconstants: $2 + (-4) = -2$\n\nResult: $5x^2 + 2x - 2$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice B ($5x^2 - 2x + 5$): You get this by dropping the negative on $-4$, computing $2 + 4 = 4$ instead of $2 + (-4) = -2$. College Board knows sign errors on constants are the most common polynomial mistake.\n- Choice C ($5x^4 + 2x^2 - 2$): You get this by multiplying exponents ($x^2 \\cdot x^2 = x^4$) instead of adding coefficients. This traps students who confuse adding polynomials with multiplying them.\n- Choice D ($6x^2 + 2x - 2$): Simple arithmetic slip: $3 + 2 = 5$ instead of $6$. Under time pressure, even basic addition can go wrong.\n\n**Verification:** At $x = 1$: $3(1) + 2(1) - 2 = 6$ and $(3 - 3 + 2) + (2 + 6 - 4) = 0 + 3 = 7$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When adding polynomials, line up like terms vertically on scratch paper. Add COEFFICIENTS only -- never touch the exponents.",
