    "question": "The system of equations below has solution $(x, y)$. determine the value of $x$?\n\n$3x + 2y = 25$\n$x - 2y = 7$",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: System of Equations by Elimination** -- 2-3 per test. When you see opposite coefficients, add the equations immediately.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (16 seconds):** The $+2y$ and $-2y$ cancel when you add. $3x + x = 4x$ and $18 + 3 = 32$. So $4x = 19$, meaning $x = 7$. Or type both equations into Desmos and read the intersection point.\n\n**Step-by-step solution:**\n$3x + 2y = 14$\n$x - 2y = 6$\n\nAdd the equations ($+2y$ and $-2y$ cancel):\n$4x = 19$\n$x = 4$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($x = 2$): Plugging in gives $8 + 2y = 16$ so $y = 4.5$, but $2 - 11 = -8 \\neq 6$. This fails the second equation. You likely made a division error somewhere.\n- Choice B ($x = 4$): Plugging in gives $8 + 2y = 25$ so $y = 3.5$, but $4 - 5 = -3 \\neq 6$. College Board includes nearby values to catch careless arithmetic.\n- Choice D ($x = 9$): Plugging in gives $23 + 2y = 22$ so $y = -2.5$, but $10 + 6 = 16 \\neq 7$. You may have made an error doubling $x = 4$ or miscombining terms.\n\n**Verification:** $x = 5$: From equation 2, $4 - 2y = 6$ so $y = 0.5$. Check equation 1: $3(8) + 2(0.5) = 20 + 1 = 21$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Before solving a system, scan the coefficients. If one variable has equal-and-opposite coefficients, just add the equations -- it takes 10 seconds and eliminates that variable instantly.",
