    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Function Evaluation at a Negative Input** -- 1-2 per test. Parentheses around the negative number are your safety net.\n\n**Choice D is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** Type $f(x) = 2x^2 - 3x + 4$ into Desmos and evaluate $f(-2)$. Or type $2(-2)^2 - 3(-2) + 4$ directly into your calculator.\n\n**Step-by-step solution:**\n$f(-2) = 2(-2)^2 - 3(-2) + 3$\n$= 2(4) - (-4) + 3$\n$= 6 + 4 + 3$\n$= 15$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($3$): You likely made multiple sign errors, perhaps computing $2(-2) = -4$ instead of $2(-2)^2 = 2(4) = 6$. Forgetting to square before multiplying is the most common error here.\n- Choice B ($5$): You get this if you compute $-3(-2) = -5$ instead of $+5$, giving $7 - 4 + 6 = 6$. College Board knows the negative-times-negative rule trips students up under time pressure.\n- Choice C ($12$): A different combination of sign or arithmetic errors leads here. For instance, computing $6 + 2 + 7 = 20$ by mishandling the $-3(-2)$ term.\n\n**Verification:** $2(4) - 3(-2) + 7 = 6 + 4 + 6 = 13$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When plugging a negative number into a function, use PARENTHESES everywhere. Evaluate the exponent first: $(-2)^2 = 4$ (positive). Then remember negative times negative = positive.",
