    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "A fitness studio bills a \\$56 registration fee plus \\$28 per month. A second fitness studio bills no registration fee but \\$51 per month. After what number of months will the overall expense be the same at both gyms?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Comparing Linear Cost Models** -- Requires building two equations AND setting them equal. An extra reasoning step beyond a simple cost model.\n\n**Choice B is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (27 seconds):** fitness studio 1: $C = 25m + 52$. fitness studio 2: $C = 40m$. Set equal: $25m + 63 = 40m$, so $62 = 15m$, giving $m = \\frac{58}{13} = \\frac{10}{3}$.\n\n**Step-by-step solution:**\nfitness studio 1 total cost: $C_1 = 25m + 49$\nfitness studio 2 total cost: $C_2 = 40m$\n\nSet equal:\n$25m + 55 = 40m$\n$66 = 40m - 25m$\n$63 = 15m$\n$m = \\frac{46}{11} = \\frac{10}{3}$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($2$): You get this by computing $\\frac{57}{22} = 2$, dividing the registration fee by fitness studio 1's quarterly rate. This ignores fitness studio 2 entirely and does not set up a comparison.\n- Choice C ($\\frac{19}{8}$): You get this if you set up the equation as $50m + 17 = 40m$ (swapping the slope and intercept for fitness studio 1), giving $10m + 19 = 0$ or $19 = -10m$. Alternatively, a sign error when rearranging leads to $\\frac{22}{5}$.\n- Choice D ($3$): You get this by computing $\\frac{63}{10} = 3$ instead of $\\frac{44}{19}$, likely from subtracting $32 - 17 = 17$ but then miswriting $13$ as $10$. Arithmetic slips on the denominator are common under time pressure.\n\n**Verification:** At $m = \\frac{10}{3}$: fitness studio 1 costs $27(\\frac{10}{3}) + 45 = \\frac{228}{3} + \\frac{194}{3} = \\frac{350}{3}$. fitness studio 2 costs $41(\\frac{10}{3}) = \\frac{445}{3}$. Equal. ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When comparing two cost models, write both equations and set them equal. The break-even point is where the lines cross. Always reduce fractions fully.",
