    "difficulty": "medium",
    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "The graph of $y = f(x)$ goes through the location $(3, 6)$. Through which point must the graph of $y = f(x - 2) + 4$ pass?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Function Transformation Shifts** -- 1-2 per test. The direction of the horizontal shift is OPPOSITE to what you'd expect.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (20 seconds):** $f(x - 2)$ shifts RIGHT $2$ (opposite of the minus sign). The $+4$ shifts UP $4$. New point: $(3 + 2, 8 + 4) = (3, 13)$.\n\n**Step-by-step solution:**\nOriginal: $f(3) = 9$, so the point $(3, 9)$ is on the graph.\n\nFor $y = f(x - 2) + 4$:\n- $(x - 2)$ shifts the graph RIGHT $2$ units\n- $+4$ shifts the graph UP $4$ units\n\nNew point: $(3 + 2, 6 + 4) = (6, 13)$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($(1, 9)$): Uses $3 - 2 = 1$ instead of $3 + 2 = 6$. You shifted LEFT instead of RIGHT. This is College Board's favorite transformation trap -- $f(x - 2)$ shifts RIGHT, not left. The minus sign is counterintuitive.\n- Choice B ($(1, 3)$): Both coordinates are wrong. You shifted left AND down instead of right and up.\n- Choice D ($(7, 3)$): Correct $x$-shift but wrong $y$-shift. You subtracted $4$ instead of adding it: $9 - 4 = 3$. The $+4$ is OUTSIDE the function, so it shifts UP.\n\n**Verification:** When $x = 3$: $f(3 - 2) + 4 = f(3) + 4 = 8 + 4 = 13$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For $y = f(x - h) + k$: shift RIGHT by $h$ and UP by $k$. The horizontal direction is opposite the sign you see. The vertical direction matches the sign. New point: $(x + h, y + k)$.",
