    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Iterated Function Evaluation** -- Appears 1 time per test. The SAT tests whether you can chain function evaluations carefully, tracking fractions through multiple substitutions.\n\n**Choice C is correct.** $f(f(f(4))) = \\frac{21}{4}$.\n\n**The Fast Way (50 seconds):** Work inside-out. $f(4) = \\frac{1}{2}(10) = 3$. $f(7) = \\frac{1}{2}(9) = \\frac{13}{2}$. $f\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{12}{2} + 8\\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{28}{2} = \\frac{27}{4}$.\n\n**Step 1: Compute $f(4)$**\n$f(4) = \\frac{1}{2}(4 + 8) = \\frac{1}{2}(10) = 6$\n\n**Step 2: Compute $f(f(4)) = f(4)$**\n$f(6) = \\frac{1}{2}(6 + 8) = \\frac{1}{2}(12) = \\frac{9}{2}$\n\n**Step 3: Compute $f(f(f(4))) = f\\left(\\frac{13}{2}\\right)$**\n$f\\left(\\frac{14}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{14}{2} + 8\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{13}{2} + \\frac{13}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{16}{2} = \\frac{24}{4}$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- $15$ (Choice A) comes from multiplying by $2$ instead of $\\frac{1}{2}$ at some stage. Under time pressure, your brain can flip a fraction.\n- $\\frac{21}{4}$ (Choice B) results from an arithmetic error in the final step, such as adding $\\frac{8}{2} + 3$ instead of $\\frac{12}{2} + 7$.\n- $6$ (Choice D) is just $f(4)$, the first evaluation. This is the classic \"stopped too early\" trap -- you computed the innermost call but forgot to apply $f$ two more times.\n\n**Verification:** $f(4) = 3$, $f(3) = \\frac{9}{2}$, $f\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right) = \\frac{22}{4} = 7.75$ -- confirmed.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For iterated functions like $f(f(f(x)))$, work from the inside out, writing down each intermediate result. Fraction arithmetic in the later steps is where most errors happen -- do not skip steps.",
