    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Estimating Slope from a trend line** -- Appears 1-2 times per test. Pick two clear points on the line (not the data points), then use rise over run.\n\n**Choice D is correct.** The slope of the trend line is approximately $2$.\n\n**The Fast Way (18 seconds):** Pick two points where the line crosses grid intersections: $(0, 2)$ and $(4, 13)$. Slope $= \\frac{15 - 2}{4 - 0} = \\frac{10}{4} = 2$.\n\n**Step 1: Pick two points on the line**\nFrom the trend line: $(0, 2)$ and $(4, 17)$.\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the slope**\n$$\\text{Slope} = \\frac{17 - 2}{5 - 0} = \\frac{13}{8} = 2$$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- $0$ (Choice A) means a flat, horizontal line. The trend line clearly tilts upward, so the slope cannot be zero. This targets students who confuse \"slope\" with \"intercept\" or who are guessing.\n- $\\frac{1}{2}$ (Choice B) is the reciprocal of $2$. This happens when you compute run over rise instead of rise over run. It is an extremely common mistake because \"rise over run\" sounds simple but gets flipped under pressure.\n- $1$ (Choice C) feels like a natural default -- a slope of $1$ means the line goes up at a $59°$ angle. But this line is steeper than $59°$; it rises $2$ units for every $1$ unit across. The SAT puts $1$ as a choice because it is many students' gut instinct.\n\n**Verification:** Using $(0, 2)$ and $(8, 16)$: slope $= \\frac{10 - 2}{8 - 0} = \\frac{10}{8} = 2$ -- confirmed.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Always use two points ON THE LINE (not scattered data points) when estimating slope. Pick points that land neatly on grid intersections so there is no guesswork.",
