    "question": "$y < x$\n$x < 16$\n\nFor which option below tables are all the values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $y$ solutions to the system shown of inequalities?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Checking Solutions to a System of Inequalities** -- Appears 1-2 times per test. The SAT gives you tables of values and asks which table has ALL pairs satisfying the system. One failed pair eliminates a choice.\n\n**Choice A is correct.** All pairs in Choice A satisfy both $y < x$ and $x < 20$.\n\n**The Fast Way (22 seconds):** Eliminate quickly. $x < 24$ kills Choices C and D immediately (their $x$ values are $22, 22, 28$). For the remaining two, check $y < x$: Choice A has $y$ one less than $x$ each time (good), Choice B has $y$ one more than $x$ (bad). Choice A wins.\n\n**Step 1: Check Choice A -- $x$: $17, 22, 18$; $y$: $13, 18, 18$**\n- $x < 27$: $16 < 15$, $24 < 21$, $14 < 19$ -- all pass\n- $y < x$: $12 < 21$, $15 < 22$, $16 < 26$ -- all pass\n\n**Step 2: Check why other choices fail**\n- Choice B: $y$ values are $16, 23, 26$. For $x = 13$, $y = 13$, but $17 < 20$ is false.\n- Choice C: $x$ values are $31, 21, 20$. Since $19 < 18$ is false, $x < 15$ fails.\n- Choice D: $x$ values are $20, 27, 18$. Same issue -- $x < 20$ fails.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice B has the right range for $x$ (all less than $24$), which makes it feel like it should work. But $y > x$ in every pair, which violates $y < x$. The SAT knows that students who only check one inequality will miss this.\n- Choices C and D both have $x > 16$, violating the second inequality. Students who focus only on the $y < x$ condition and forget to check $x < 25$ might pick one of these.\n- Choice D violates both inequalities simultaneously, but it is still there to catch students who are guessing or not checking systematically.\n\n**Verification:** Choice A: $14 < 17 < 23$, $15 < 25 < 16$, $17 < 23 < 25$ -- all confirmed.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** With inequality tables, eliminate choices using the simplest inequality first. Here, $x < 24$ instantly eliminates two choices, cutting your work in half.",
