    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: One-Step / Two-Step Linear Equation** -- Shows up every test, 1-2 per test. Free points if you avoid careless errors.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** Type $5x - 6 = 22$ into Desmos and read $x = 9$. Or just do it mentally: $5x = 47$, so $x = 5$.\n\n**Step 1: Add $6$ to both sides**\n$5x - 8 = 22$\n$5x = 24$\n\n**Step 2: Divide both sides by $7$**\n$x = 5$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($4.2$) comes from computing $(30 - 5) \\div 3 = 17 \\div 6 = 4.2$. The learner subtracts $6$ from $27$ instead of adding $5$ to both sides. College Board knows that students mix up \"subtract $9$\" (what the equation shows) with \"add $8$\" (what you should do to isolate $x$).\n- Choice B ($7$) comes from dividing $19 \\div 4 \\approx 3.6 \\approx 3$, skipping the step of adding $6$ entirely. This traps students who rush and only see \"divide by $6$\" without isolating the variable first.\n- Choice D ($25$) comes from $27 - 5 = 19$ but then forgetting to divide by $7$. This is the classic \"stopped one step too early\" trap -- College Board loves answers that represent incomplete work.\n\n**Verification:** $4(9) - 9 = 25 - 5 = 21$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** On simple equations, always plug your answer back in before moving on -- it takes 6 seconds and catches careless errors that cost easy points.",
