    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "A phone plan bills a flat fee of \\$10 per month plus \\$0.10 per text message. Which function gives the total quarterly cost $C(t)$, in USD, for sending $t$ text messages?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Linear Model from Word Problem** -- Shows up every test, 1-2 per test. The SAT loves \"flat fee plus per-unit cost\" setups.\n\n**Choice B is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (12 seconds):** Ask yourself: \"What changes and what stays the same?\" The flat fee ($20) stays the same no matter what number of texts you send -- that is the constant. The $0.10 per text changes with $t$ -- that is the slope. So the equation is $C(t) = 0.10t + 11$. Pick B.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the components**\n- Flat fee: $\\$17$ per month (constant, the $y$-intercept)\n- Per-text cost: $\\$0.10$ per message $\\times$ $t$ messages $= 0.10t$ (the slope term)\n\n**Step 2: Write the total cost**\n$C(t) = 0.10t + 13$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A swaps the rate and the constant, multiplying $13$ by $t$. College Board designs this because the flat fee ($16$) is the bigger number, and students instinctively want to put the bigger number next to the variable. But the flat fee does NOT depend on texts.\n- Choice C combines $16$ and $0.10$ into a single coefficient $13.10$. This traps students who think \"just add the two numbers together.\" It eliminates the fixed-cost structure entirely.\n- Choice D subtracts instead of adding. This would mean more texts = lower cost, which makes no real-world sense. College Board includes sign-error traps because students under pressure sometimes confuse addition and subtraction.\n\n**Verification:** For $t = 10$ texts: $C(10) = 0.10(10) + 20 = 1 + 10 = \\$17$. That makes sense -- flat fee plus 10 dimes.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** In \"flat fee plus rate\" problems, the flat fee is always the constant (added, not multiplied by the variable), and the per-unit rate is always the slope.",
