    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "$3x + 2y = 13$\n$x - 2y = -3$\n\nfind the solution $(x, y)$ to the system shown of equations?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: System of Linear Equations** -- Shows up every test, 1-3 per test.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (17 seconds):** In Desmos, type both equations: $3x + 2y = 22$ and $x - 2y = -3$. Click the intersection point. It reads $(4, 3.5)$. Pick A. Alternatively, notice the $+2y$ and $-2y$ cancel perfectly -- just add the equations.\n\n**Step 1: Add the equations (elimination)**\n$(3x + 2y) + (x - 2y) = 13 + (-3)$\n$4x = 20$\n$x = 4$\n\n**Step 2: Substitute $x = 4$ into the second equation**\n$4 - 2y = -3$\n$-2y = -9$\n$y = 3.5$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice B $(3, 2)$: Satisfies the first equation ($3(6) + 2(2) = 15$) but NOT the second ($3 - 2(2) = 1 \\neq -3$). College Board designs all the wrong answers to satisfy the first equation. This exploits students who only check one equation and assume they are done.\n- Choice C $(3, 7)$: Also satisfies only the first equation ($3(3) + 2(6) = 21$, but $3 - 2(3) = -9 \\neq -3$). Same trap.\n- Choice D $(2, 4.5)$: Same pattern ($3(2) + 2(8.5) = 18$, but $2 - 2(7.5) = -12 \\neq -3$). College Board found all integer/half-integer pairs that solve the first equation and made them the wrong answers.\n\n**Verification:** $3(4) + 2(3.5) = 15 + 8 = 17$ and $4 - 2(3.5) = 4 - 8 = -3$. Both equations check out.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When the coefficients of one variable are already opposites (like $+2y$ and $-2y$), add the equations immediately -- do not waste time with substitution. And always verify in BOTH equations.",
