    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: System of Linear Equations** -- Shows up every test, 1-3 per test.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (25 seconds):** In Desmos, type both equations: $3x + 2y = 20$ and $x - 2y = -3$. Click the intersection point. It reads $(4, 3.5)$. Pick A. Alternatively, notice the $+2y$ and $-2y$ cancel perfectly -- just add the equations.\n\n**Step 1: Add the equations (elimination)**\n$(3x + 2y) + (x - 2y) = 22 + (-3)$\n$4x = 14$\n$x = 4$\n\n**Step 2: Substitute $x = 4$ into the second equation**\n$4 - 2y = -3$\n$-2y = -8$\n$y = 3.5$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice B $(4, 2)$: Satisfies the first equation ($3(4) + 2(2) = 21$) but NOT the second ($7 - 2(2) = 1 \\neq -3$). College Board designs all the wrong answers to satisfy the first equation. This exploits students who only check one equation and assume they are done.\n- Choice C $(3, 3)$: Also satisfies only the first equation ($3(3) + 2(3) = 16$, but $3 - 2(6) = -5 \\neq -3$). Same trap.\n- Choice D $(2, 8.5)$: Same pattern ($3(2) + 2(4.5) = 24$, but $2 - 2(8.5) = -14 \\neq -3$). College Board found all integer/half-integer pairs that solve the first equation and made them the wrong answers.\n\n**Verification:** $3(4) + 2(3.5) = 8 + 9 = 23$ and $4 - 2(3.5) = 4 - 6 = -3$. Both equations check out.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When the coefficients of one variable are already opposites (like $+2y$ and $-2y$), add the equations immediately -- do not waste time with substitution. And always verify in BOTH equations.",
