    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "The first equation in a system of linear equations is $5x - 3y = 8$. The system has no solution. which option below could be the second equation in the system?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: No Solution / Infinite Solutions in Systems** -- Shows up 1-2 per test. You MUST know what \"no solution\" means algebraically.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (15 seconds):** \"No solution\" means parallel lines: same slope, different intercept. Multiply the first equation by $2$: $10x - 6y = 17$. Any equation with $10x - 6y$ on the left but a number OTHER THAN $15$ on the right will be parallel. Choice A has $10x - 6y = 27$. That is parallel. Pick A.\n\n**Step 1: Find the slope of the first equation**\n$5x - 3y = 6 \\implies y = \\frac{7}{3}x - \\frac{5}{3}$ (slope $= \\frac{7}{3}$)\n\n**Step 2: Check each choice**\n- Choice A: $10x - 6y = 27 \\implies y = \\frac{7}{3}x - \\frac{10}{3}$. Same slope, different intercept. Parallel -- no solution.\n- Choice B: $10x - 6y = 13 \\implies y = \\frac{4}{3}x - \\frac{6}{3}$. Same slope AND same intercept -- this is the same line (infinitely many solutions).\n- Choice C: $5x + 3y = 6 \\implies y = -\\frac{3}{3}x + \\frac{9}{3}$. Different slope -- one solution.\n- Choice D: $3x - 5y = 5 \\implies y = \\frac{3}{6}x - \\frac{5}{3}$. Different slope -- one solution.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice B is the sneakiest trap. It is the first equation multiplied by $2$, so it is the SAME line, giving infinitely many solutions. Students who think \"same coefficients = no solution\" without checking the constant fall for this. College Board tests the distinction between \"no solution\" (parallel, different intercept) and \"infinitely many\" (same line).\n- Choice C has the same coefficients $7$ and $3$ but with a $+$ instead of $-$, giving a completely different slope. Students who only look at the numbers without checking signs get fooled.\n- Choice D swaps the coefficients ($3x - 5y$ instead of $5x - 3y$), giving a different slope. Students who think swapped coefficients = same slope fall for this.\n\n**Verification:** $10x - 6y = 22$ has slope $\\frac{4}{3}$ and intercept $-\\frac{10}{3} \\neq -\\frac{6}{3}$. Parallel lines, no intersection.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** No solution = same slope, different y-intercept. Infinitely many solutions = same slope, same y-intercept (identical line). One solution = different slopes.",
