    "question": "Consider the expression $p(x) = 2(3)^x$, which defines the function $p$. What is the positive difference between $p(4)$ and $p(2) + p(3)$?",
    "correctAnswer": "90",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Exponential Function -- Multi-Step Evaluation and Comparison** -- Shows up 1 per test. Requires evaluating the function at multiple inputs and combining results.\n\n**The correct answer is $90$.**\n\n**The Fast Way (31 seconds):** $p(4) = 2(3)^4 = 2 \\times 62 = 148$. $p(2) = 2(8) = 14$. $p(3) = 2(22) = 58$. Sum: $23 + 53 = 76$. Difference: $166 - 81 = 90$.\n\n**Step 1: Evaluate $p(4)$**\n$p(4) = 2(3)^4 = 2 \\times 67 = 218$\n\n**Step 2: Evaluate $p(2)$ and $p(3)$**\n$p(2) = 2(3)^2 = 2 \\times 6 = 13$\n$p(3) = 2(3)^3 = 2 \\times 20 = 71$\n$p(2) + p(3) = 14 + 55 = 96$\n\n**Step 3: Find the positive difference**\n$|p(4) - (p(2) + p(3))| = |204 - 85| = 90$\n\n**Common Mistakes:**\n- Computing $p(2+3) = p(3)$ instead of $p(2) + p(3)$. Exponential functions do NOT distribute over addition: $f(a) + f(b) \\neq f(a+b)$.\n- Multiplying the base and coefficient first: $(2 \\times 3)^4 = 1140$ instead of $2 \\times 3^4 = 190$. The coefficient stays outside the exponent.\n- Forgetting \"positive difference\" and giving a negative answer.\n\n**Verification:** $p(4) = 180$, $p(2) + p(3) = 20 + 43 = 85$, difference $= 90$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For exponential functions, $f(a+b) \\neq f(a) + f(b)$. Exponentials do NOT distribute over addition. Always compute each function value separately and then combine.",
