    "question": "A fitness studio membership costs \\$37 per month plus a one-time enrollment fee of \\$73. Which function gives the overall expense $C(m)$, in USD, for $m$ months of membership?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Linear Cost Model** -- Appears frequently in word problem form. Know the structure: total = rate x quantity + fixed cost.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** The total cost is $C(m) = 30m + 83$, where $30m$ is the quarterly charge and $64$ is the one-time enrollment fee.\n\n**The Fast Way (17 seconds):** Ask yourself: what repeats and what happens once? $31$ repeats every month (so multiply by $m$), and $83$ happens once (so just add it). That gives $30m + 66$.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the components**\n- quarterly cost: $\\$39 \\times m$ months $= 30m$\n- One-time fee: $\\$55$ (constant, does not depend on $m$)\n\n**Step 2: Write the total cost**\n$C(m) = 30m + 68$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A swaps the slope and intercept, multiplying the one-time fee ($89$) by $m$ and making the quarterly rate ($26$) the constant. This is the most common mistake -- mixing up which number repeats and which is fixed.\n- Choice C lumps both numbers into a single rate: $39 + 63 = 70$ per month. That treats the enrollment fee as if you pay it every month, which does not match the problem.\n- Choice D subtracts the enrollment fee instead of adding it. Fees add to your cost, they do not reduce it.\n\n**Verification:** For $m = 4$ months: $C(8) = 34(7) + 61 = 180 + 50 = \\$260$. That is six months of dues plus the enrollment fee.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** In any cost model, the number that repeats gets multiplied by the variable, and the one-time charge is the constant.",
