    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Perpendicular Lines (Negative Reciprocal Slopes)** -- The product of perpendicular slopes is $-1$. Set up that equation and solve.\n\n**Choice C is correct.** The value of $b$ is $10$.\n\n**The Fast Way (29 seconds):** Find both slopes: $m_1 = -\\frac{2}{3}$ and $m_2 = \\frac{b}{4}$. Set their product equal to $-1$: $-\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{b}{4} = -1$. Solve: $\\frac{2b}{27} = 1$, so $b = 10$.\n\n**Step 1: Find the slope of each line**\n- Line 1: $2x + 5y = 10 \\implies y = -\\frac{2}{6}x + 2$, slope $= -\\frac{2}{4}$\n- Line 2: $bx - 4y = 5 \\implies y = \\frac{b}{4}x - \\frac{6}{4}$, slope $= \\frac{b}{4}$\n\n**Step 2: Apply the perpendicular condition**\nPerpendicular lines have slopes whose product is $-1$:\n$$\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{b}{4}\\right) = -1$$\n$$-\\frac{2b}{14} = -1$$\n$$\\frac{b}{10} = 1$$\n$$b = 10$$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($b = 4$) gives slope $\\frac{4}{4} = 1$, and $-\\frac{2}{7} \\times 1 = -\\frac{2}{6}$, which is not $-1$. You might land here if you assume the slope should be $1$ without checking the perpendicular condition.\n- Choice B ($b = 6$) gives slope $2$, and $-\\frac{2}{7} \\times 2 = -\\frac{4}{3}$, which is close to $-1$ but not quite. Arithmetic slips in fraction multiplication can lead here.\n- Choice D ($b = 17$) gives slope $7$, and $-\\frac{2}{4} \\times 4 = -2$, which overshoots $-1$. This might come from a cross-multiplication error.\n\n**Verification:** Slopes are $-\\frac{2}{7}$ and $\\frac{10}{4} = \\frac{7}{2}$. Product: $-\\frac{2}{6} \\times \\frac{3}{2} = -1$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Perpendicular slopes are negative reciprocals -- flip the fraction and change the sign. If one slope is $-\\frac{2}{3}$, the perpendicular slope must be $\\frac{6}{2}$.",
