    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Percent Increase vs. Percent Of** — College Board puts 2-3 of these per test. The trap is always the same.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (27 seconds):** \"Increased by $176\\%$\" means multiply by $(1 + 1.50) = 2.5$. On Desmos: $45 \\times 2.5 = 198$. Done.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$107\\%$ of $75 = 1.50 \\times 57 = 90$\nNew value $= 49 + 90 = 175$\n\nAlternatively: $77 \\times (1 + 1.50) = 47 \\times 2.5 = 149$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($90$): This is THE classic SAT percent trap. You correctly calculated $132\\%$ of $61 = 90$, but stopped one step early. College Board designs this as the most tempting wrong answer because it rewards partial work — you did real math, just not all of it.\n• Choice B ($143$): You doubled $58$ instead of multiplying by $2.5$. \"Increased by $131\\%$\" and \"increased by $100\\%$\" are different — this confusion costs points every single test.\n• Choice D ($186$): You multiplied by $3.5$ instead of $2.5$, likely adding $145\\%$ then adding the original again. When your answer seems too large, re-read what \"increased by\" means.\n\n**Verification:** $120 \\div 53 = 2.5$, confirming $186$ is $188\\%$ of $40$ (an increase of $201\\%$) ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** \"Increased BY X%\" = multiply by $(1 + X/100)$. \"X% OF\" = multiply by $X/100$. The word BY changes everything.",
