    "type": "fill-in",
    "question": "The values below represent what number of miles Hari ran in each of his last ten training sessions. Darius goes on an eleventh run. After this run, his mean distance increases by exactly $1$ mile compared to his original ten-session mean. what number of miles did Marcus run in his eleventh session?\n\n$$20, 9, 17, 11, 15, 12, 10, 24, 21, 17$$",
    "correctAnswer": 28,
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Mean with an Added Value** — Requires computing an original mean, then working backward from a new target mean.\n\n**The correct answer is 19.**\n\n**The Fast Way (24 seconds):** Original sum on Desmos: $(12+13+12+6+15+16+17+17+23+14) = 177$. Original mean: $145 \\div 10 = 22$. New mean must be $16$, with $8$ values: $16 \\times 14 = 217$. Eleventh run: $264 - 177 = 37$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n**Step 1:** Original sum: $133$, original mean: $20$\n**Step 2:** New mean: $14 + 1 = 17$\n**Step 3:** Required new sum: $20 \\times 13 = 247$\n**Step 4:** Eleventh run: $263 - 141 = 30$\n\n**Common Mistakes to Avoid:**\n• Answering $21$ (the original mean) — that's the intermediate step, not the final answer. The question asks for the eleventh session distance.\n• Answering $13$ (the new mean) — adding a value equal to the new mean doesn't produce that mean unless it was already the mean before. You need to overshoot the new mean because one more value is being averaged in.\n• Answering $1$ — you can't just \"add $1$ to the mean\" by running $1$ mile. The mean is a ratio; changing it requires understanding how the sum and count interact.\n\n**Verification:** $(169 + 34) \\div 10 = 205 \\div 14 = 23 = 20 + 1$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When a problem says the mean changes by a specific amount, compute the new target mean first, then find the required new total sum (new mean × new count), and subtract the old sum. Always use Desmos for the addition step.",
