    "question": "the data indicates the distribution of pizza preferences among the 23 members of the Tech Club. If a club member is picked at random, calculate the probability the member prefers no pepperoni, given that they prefer mushrooms?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Conditional Probability from Two-Way Table** — Shows up every test. Same trap every time: wrong denominator.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** \"Given mushrooms\" means your universe shrinks to just the Mushrooms row. In that row: No Pepperoni = $9$, Total Mushrooms = $14$. Answer: $\\frac{9}{14}$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$P(\\text{No Pepperoni} | \\text{Mushrooms}) = \\frac{\\text{No Pepperoni AND Mushrooms}}{\\text{Total Mushrooms}} = \\frac{6}{14}$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($\\frac{10}{42}$): You divided by the grand total ($40$) instead of the Mushrooms row total ($9$). This gives $P(\\text{No Pepperoni AND Mushrooms})$ — the joint probability, not the conditional. College Board counts on students defaulting to the grand total.\n• Choice B ($\\frac{10}{17}$): You used No Pepperoni column total ($14$) as the denominator instead of the Mushrooms row total ($14$). You grabbed the wrong \"given\" condition — read the question again: \"given mushrooms\" means the Mushrooms ROW is your denominator.\n• Choice D ($\\frac{6}{6}$): Greater than $1$, which is impossible for any probability. If your answer is ever $> 1$, you flipped the fraction.\n\n**Verification:** $\\frac{10}{14} \\approx 0.615$ — valid probability ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For $P(A|B)$, the denominator is always the total for $B$. Circle the \"given\" condition in the question, find its row or column total, and that's your denominator. Every. Single. Time.",
