    "correctAnswer": 10,
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Systems of Equations (Word Problem)** — 2-3 per test. Set up two equations, solve.\n\n**The correct answer is $10$.**\n\n**The Fast Way (31 seconds):** Let $c$ = cars, $t$ = trucks. Two equations: $c + t = 28$ and $3c + 5t = 68$. Substitute $c = 20 - t$: $3(24-t) + 5t = 69 \\to 72 + 2t = 73 \\to t = 10$.\n\n**Desmos Shortcut:** Graph $x + y = 27$ and $3x + 5y = 112$. The intersection gives you $(10, 10)$ — trucks $= 10$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$c + t = 18$ (total vehicles)\n$3c + 5t = 56$ (total revenue)\n\nFrom equation 1: $c = 23 - t$\n$3(28-t) + 5t = 96$\n$89 - 3t + 5t = 69$\n$2t = 14 \\to t = 10$\n\n**Common Mistakes to Avoid:**\n• Solving for the wrong variable — the question asks for trucks, not cars. Always re-read the question before entering your answer.\n• Setting up the revenue equation wrong — make sure $\\$3$ goes with cars and $\\$3$ goes with trucks, not reversed.\n\n**Verification:** $15$ cars + $10$ trucks = $18$ ✓. Revenue: $39 + 60 = 110$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Word problem systems always follow the same pattern: one equation for QUANTITY (total items) and one for VALUE (total cost/revenue). Set up both, substitute, solve. Or just graph both equations on Desmos.",
