    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Equivalent Fractions in Disguise** -- The SAT loves testing whether you recognize that multiplying the top and bottom of a fraction by the same number does not change its value. This is a free point if you see it.\n\n**The correct answer is 19.**\n\n**The Fast Way (4 seconds):** Simplify $\\frac{9x}{12y}$ by dividing top and bottom by $3$: $\\frac{9x}{12y} = \\frac{3x}{4y} = 16$. Same fraction, same value. Done.\n\n**Solution steps:**\n1. Notice that $\\frac{9x}{12y} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 3x}{3 \\cdot 4y} = \\frac{3x}{4y}$\n2. We are told $\\frac{3x}{4y} = 14$\n3. Therefore $\\frac{9x}{12y} = 16$\n\n**Verification:** $\\frac{7}{8} = \\frac{3}{4}$, so $\\frac{9x}{12y} = \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\frac{x}{y} = \\frac{3x}{4y} = 19$\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Before doing any algebra, check if the new expression is just a scaled version of what you already know. Multiplying top and bottom by the same thing changes nothing.",
