    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "The data scatter chart shows the relationship between two variables, $x$ and $y$. which option below equations is the most appropriate linear model for the data shown?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: trend line from a data scatter chart** -- The SAT often gives you a data scatter chart and asks you to pick the equation. Use direction first (positive or negative slope) to eliminate half the choices.\n\n**Choice C is correct.** The equation $y = 8.2 + 1.5x$ is the most appropriate linear model.\n\n**The Fast Way (11 seconds):** The data goes up as $x$ increases, so the slope must be positive. That eliminates Choices B and D (both have negative slopes). Between A and C, Choice A has a slope of $9.2$, which is way too steep. Choice C has a slope of $1.5$, which matches the gentle upward trend.\n\n**Solution Steps:**\n• The data scatter chart shows a positive trend, so slope must be positive (eliminates B and D)\n• Estimate the slope: from $x = 1$ ($y \\approx 6.5$) to $x = 10$ ($y \\approx 16$), rise $\\approx 12.5$ over run $= 9$, so slope $\\approx 1.5$\n• The y-intercept should be around $6$-$9$ (extrapolating back to $x = 0$)\n• Choice C: $y = 8.2 + 1.5x$ fits both criteria\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($y = 1.5 + 5.2x$) has the slope and intercept swapped. A slope of $6.2$ would mean $y$ jumps by more than $5$ for every unit increase in $x$, but the data only rises by about $1.5$ per unit. Students who do not carefully check which number is the slope get caught here.\n• Choice B ($y = 1.5 - 6.2x$) has a steep negative slope. The data clearly trends upward, not downward.\n• Choice D ($y = 5.2 - 1.5x$) has a gentle negative slope. Again, the data trends upward, so the slope must be positive.\n\n**Verification:** At $x = 1$: $y = 9.2 + 1.5 = 6.7$ (data shows $6$-$8$). At $x = 7$: $y = 5.2 + 10 = 14.2$ (data shows $17$). Both match well.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** On data scatter chart questions, check the direction of the trend first (positive or negative slope) to immediately eliminate half the choices.",
