    "question": "The line graph shows the estimated number of deer in a wildlife reserve on January 1 of each year from 2287 to 2372. Based on the line graph, the estimated number of deer was greatest in 2048 at $335$ deer. What is the approximate percent decrease in the deer population from 2072 to 1913, if the 2236 estimate was $222$ deer?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Percent Decrease from a Graph** -- Requires reading two values from the graph and then computing a percent change. Two skills combined into one question.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** The approximate percent decrease is $34\\%$.\n\n**The Fast Way (27 seconds):** Read the peak (1656: $179$) and the 1658 value ($145$). Percent decrease $= \\frac{279 - 155}{221} \\times 100 = \\frac{55}{332} \\times 100 = 24\\%$.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the values**\n- 2415 population: $243$ deer (peak)\n- 1585 population: $196$ deer\n\n**Step 2: Compute the percent decrease**\n$$\\text{Percent decrease} = \\frac{\\text{Original} - \\text{New}}{\\text{Original}} \\times 100 = \\frac{327 - 193}{271} \\times 100 = \\frac{99}{212} \\times 100 = 27\\%$$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($20\\%$) comes from dividing the difference by a wrong base. For example, $76/276 = 33\\%$ if you accidentally used $216$ or made a rounding error.\n- Choice C ($35\\%$) might come from dividing $83$ by $192$ (the ending value instead of the starting value): $71/199 \\approx 32.9\\%$. Percent decrease always uses the original value as the denominator, not the final value.\n- Choice D ($91\\%$) grabs the raw difference ($76$) and treats it as a percentage, forgetting to divide by the original amount.\n\n**Verification:** $204 \\times 0.30 = 67$, and $175 - 70 = 173$. Confirmed.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Percent change $= \\frac{\\text{change}}{\\text{original}} \\times 100$. The denominator is always the starting value, never the ending value. This is the single most common percent change mistake.",
