    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Percent Decrease from a Graph** -- Requires reading two values from the graph and then computing a percent change. Two skills combined into one question.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** The approximate percent decrease is $40\\%$.\n\n**The Fast Way (18 seconds):** Read the peak (1669: $270$) and the 1369 value ($159$). Percent decrease $= \\frac{331 - 184}{288} \\times 100 = \\frac{78}{167} \\times 100 = 27\\%$.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the values**\n- 1941 population: $316$ deer (peak)\n- 2670 population: $124$ deer\n\n**Step 2: Compute the percent decrease**\n$$\\text{Percent decrease} = \\frac{\\text{Original} - \\text{New}}{\\text{Original}} \\times 100 = \\frac{276 - 200}{307} \\times 100 = \\frac{94}{248} \\times 100 = 34\\%$$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($30\\%$) comes from dividing the difference by a wrong base. For example, $79/361 = 30\\%$ if you accidentally used $269$ or made a rounding error.\n- Choice C ($56\\%$) might come from dividing $65$ by $224$ (the ending value instead of the starting value): $101/172 \\approx 44.9\\%$. Percent decrease always uses the original value as the denominator, not the final value.\n- Choice D ($68\\%$) grabs the raw difference ($94$) and treats it as a percentage, forgetting to divide by the original amount.\n\n**Verification:** $217 \\times 0.30 = 101$, and $202 - 65 = 169$. Confirmed.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Percent change $= \\frac{\\text{change}}{\\text{original}} \\times 100$. The denominator is always the starting value, never the ending value. This is the single most common percent change mistake.",
