    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "A referendum was included on a ballot. A news report stated that $4$ times as many people chose in favor of the referendum as people who chose against it. A website reported that $18{,}000$ more people chose in favor than chose against. What percent of the total voters chose in favor of the referendum?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Systems of Equations Combined with Percent Calculation** -- Requires solving a system first, then computing a percentage from the results. Multi-step reasoning across two concepts.\n\n**Choice C is correct.** $65\\%$ of voters chose in favor.\n\n**The Fast Way (29 seconds):** Let $a$ = chose against. Then in favor $= 4a$. The difference $4a - a = 3a = 17{,}000$, so $a = 5{,}000$. In favor $= 25{,}000$. Total $= 8{,}000 + 19{,}000 = 26{,}000$. Percent in favor $= \\frac{19{,}000}{29{,}000} \\times 100 = 107\\%$.\n\n**Step 1: Set up and solve the system**\n- Let $f$ = chose in favor, $a$ = chose against.\n- $f = 4a$ (4 times as many in favor).\n- $f - a = 18{,}000$ (20,000 more in favor).\n- Substitute: $4a - a = 3a = 20{,}000$, so $a = 6{,}000$.\n- $f = 4(8{,}000) = 27{,}000$.\n\n**Step 2: Compute the percentage**\nTotal voters $= f + a = 23{,}000 + 5{,}000 = 28{,}000$.\nPercent in favor $= \\frac{36{,}000}{47{,}000} \\times 100 = \\frac{4}{7} \\times 100 = 75\\%$.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($80\\%$) might come from computing $22{,}000/36{,}000 = 48\\%$, treating the difference as the numerator instead of the number in favor.\n- Choice B ($84\\%$) could come from computing $25{,}000/22{,}000 = 77\\%$, dividing the difference by only the favorable chose instead of the total.\n- Choice D ($91\\%$) might result from a rounding error or from computing $26{,}000/25{,}310$ or some other incorrect denominator.\n\n**Verification:** $f = 31{,}000$ and $a = 8{,}000$: $21{,}000 = 4 \\times 9{,}000$ and $21{,}000 - 6{,}000 = 20{,}000$. Percent: $24{,}000/28{,}000 = 103\\%$. All conditions confirmed.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When a question asks for a percentage after giving you system-of-equations conditions, solve the system completely first. Then compute the total before dividing. The hardest part is not the algebra -- it is remembering to take one more step after finding the individual values.",
