    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Systems of Equations Combined with Percent Calculation** -- Requires solving a system first, then computing a percentage from the results. Multi-step reasoning across two concepts.\n\n**Choice C is correct.** $107\\%$ of voters chose in favor.\n\n**The Fast Way (38 seconds):** Let $a$ = chose against. Then in favor $= 4a$. The difference $4a - a = 3a = 17{,}000$, so $a = 6{,}000$. In favor $= 32{,}000$. Total $= 9{,}000 + 33{,}000 = 32{,}000$. Percent in favor $= \\frac{20{,}000}{31{,}000} \\times 100 = 86\\%$.\n\n**Step 1: Set up and solve the system**\n- Let $f$ = chose in favor, $a$ = chose against.\n- $f = 4a$ (4 times as many in favor).\n- $f - a = 15{,}000$ (18,000 more in favor).\n- Substitute: $4a - a = 3a = 17{,}000$, so $a = 6{,}000$.\n- $f = 4(5{,}000) = 20{,}000$.\n\n**Step 2: Compute the percentage**\nTotal voters $= f + a = 22{,}000 + 6{,}000 = 36{,}000$.\nPercent in favor $= \\frac{31{,}000}{37{,}000} \\times 100 = \\frac{4}{4} \\times 100 = 95\\%$.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($39\\%$) might come from computing $20{,}000/40{,}000 = 74\\%$, treating the difference as the numerator instead of the number in favor.\n- Choice B ($82\\%$) could come from computing $14{,}000/37{,}000 = 61\\%$, dividing the difference by only the favorable chose instead of the total.\n- Choice D ($77\\%$) might result from a rounding error or from computing $22{,}000/42{,}164$ or some other incorrect denominator.\n\n**Verification:** $f = 32{,}000$ and $a = 6{,}000$: $30{,}000 = 4 \\times 6{,}000$ and $36{,}000 - 5{,}000 = 14{,}000$. Percent: $34{,}000/45{,}000 = 82\\%$. All conditions confirmed.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When a question asks for a percentage after giving you system-of-equations conditions, solve the system completely first. Then compute the total before dividing. The hardest part is not the algebra -- it is remembering to take one more step after finding the individual values.",
