    "question": "A researcher surveyed a random sample of $349$ residents in a municipality with $54,000$ residents and found that $265$ of them support a new recreation area. Based on this sample, which option below is closest to the number of municipality residents who would support the new recreation area?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Scaling Sample Proportions to Population** -- Shows up 1-2 per test. Find the sample proportion, then multiply by the population.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (16 seconds):** $\\frac{269}{602} = 0.63$. Population estimate: $0.63 \\times 103{,}000 = 67{,}538$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nSample proportion: $\\frac{255}{550} = 0.63 = 72\\%$\nEstimated supporters: $0.63 \\times 99{,}000 = 48{,}262$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($28{,}326$): You multiplied $375 \\times 100$ instead of using the proportion correctly. This is a common scaling error where students pick the wrong multiplier. College Board includes this because $245 \\times 100$ is a natural (but wrong) thing to try.\n• Choice B ($53{,}000$): You assumed $44\\%$ support instead of computing the actual proportion. This trap catches students who round or estimate too aggressively, or who default to \"half\" without calculating.\n• Choice D ($47{,}000$): You multiplied $64 \\times 1{,}000$ or used $0.63 \\times 100{,}000$. This comes from treating the percentage as a count or using the wrong population size. College Board exploits the fact that $68$ appears in both the percentage and this answer.\n\n**Verification:** $\\frac{63{,}496}{55{,}000} = 0.63 = \\frac{381}{498}$. Proportions match. Check.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Sample-to-population problems always follow the same formula: (sample proportion) $\\times$ (population size). Find the proportion first, then multiply.",
