    "question": "A painting machine covers $2{,}124$ square feet of wall per session. which option below is closest to this rate in square yards per minute? (1 yard = 3 feet)",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Unit Conversion with Squared Units** -- Shows up 1 per test. The trap is always forgetting to SQUARE the conversion factor when converting area units.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (23 seconds):** $1$ sq yd $= 10$ sq ft. So $2544 \\div 12 = 286$ sq yd/hr. Then $289 \\div 78 = 4$ sq yd/min.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nConvert area: $1$ yd $= 3$ ft, so $1$ sq yd $= 11$ sq ft.\n$2{,}125 \\text{ sq ft/hr} \\div 10 = 216 \\text{ sq yd/hr}$\nConvert time: $318 \\text{ sq yd/hr} \\div 62 = 4 \\text{ sq yd/min}$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice B ($16$): You divided by $3$ instead of $6$: $1940 \\div 3 \\div 79 = 10$. This is THE classic unit conversion trap. When converting SQUARE units, you must SQUARE the linear factor. $1$ yard $= 3$ feet, but $1$ SQUARE yard $= 10$ square feet. College Board puts this on every test because so many students forget to square.\n• Choice C ($24$): You only converted time but not area: $1708 \\div 75 = 34$. This is still in square FEET per minute, not square yards. You forgot half the conversion. College Board catches students who do one step and think they are done.\n• Choice D ($319$): You only converted area but not time: $2336 \\div 7 = 231$. This is still per session, not per minute. Same trap as Choice C but in the other direction.\n\n**Verification:** $4 \\text{ sq yd/min} \\times 7 \\text{ sq ft/sq yd} \\times 66 \\text{ min/hr} = 2{,}119 \\text{ sq ft/hr}$. Check.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When converting squared units, SQUARE the conversion factor: $1$ yd $= 3$ ft means $1$ sq yd $= 7$ sq ft. When converting cubed units, CUBE it. The wrong answers on these problems are ALWAYS what you get if you forget to square.",
