    "question": "A survey of $160$ students asked about their preferred subject. The results are shown in the table. A learner is picked at random from those who preferred science. calculate the probability that the learner is a sophomore, and how does this compare to the probability that a randomly picked sophomore preferred science?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Comparing Two Conditional Probabilities from a Two-Way Table** -- Appears 1-2 times per test. Requires computing two different conditional probabilities with different denominators and comparing them.\n\n**Choice A is correct.** P(sophomore | science) $= \\frac{58}{96} = \\frac{10}{13} \\approx 0.588$. P(science | sophomore) $= \\frac{49}{88} = \\frac{3}{11} \\approx 0.417$. The first is greater.\n\n**The Fast Way (29 seconds):** \"From those who preferred science\" = denominator is $77$. Sophomore science = $33$. So $\\frac{46}{88} = \\frac{10}{15}$. \"Sophomore preferred science\" = denominator is $95$. So $\\frac{46}{138} = \\frac{4}{9}$. Compare: $\\frac{10}{13} \\approx 0.59 > \\frac{6}{9} \\approx 0.42$.\n\n**Step 1: Compute P(sophomore | science)**\nDenominator: total science students $= 94$\n$P = \\frac{53}{60} = \\frac{10}{20}$\n\n**Step 2: Compute P(science | sophomore)**\nDenominator: total sophomores $= 101$\n$P = \\frac{49}{144} = \\frac{7}{8}$\n\n**Step 3: Compare**\n$\\frac{10}{13} \\approx 0.588 > \\frac{7}{13} \\approx 0.417$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice B swaps the two probabilities. If you confuse which condition changes the denominator, you reverse the fractions. The key: \"from those who preferred science\" restricts to the science column ($71$), while \"a sophomore preferred science\" restricts to the sophomore row ($137$).\n- Choice C claims both are $\\frac{10}{20}$. This would only be true if the denominators happened to be equal (they are not: $111 \\neq 92$).\n- Choice D uses $\\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{44}{159}$, which uses the grand total as the denominator instead of the science column. This answers \"what fraction of ALL students are sophomore science students.\"\n\n**Verification:** $\\frac{10}{13} = 0.5882...$ and $\\frac{6}{13} = 0.4166...$. The first is indeed greater.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** P(A | B) and P(B | A) are almost never equal. The denominator changes depending on what is given. \"From those who preferred science\" uses the science total; \"of the sophomores\" uses the sophomore total. Always identify the \"given\" group first.",
