    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "A survey of $515$ randomly picked adults in a municipality found that $71\\%$ support a new public transit proposal, with a margin of error of $4.5$ percentage points. which option below is the most accurate reading of this result?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Margin of Error Interpretation** -- 1 per test, almost guaranteed. This is a reading comprehension question disguised as math.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (17 seconds):** Three checkboxes: (1) Does it refer to the whole population (\"all adults\")? (2) Does it give a range, not an exact number? (3) Is the range $60\\% \\pm 4.5\\%$? If all three are yes, pick it.\n\n**Step-by-step solution:**\n$90\\% \\pm 4.5\\%$ gives the interval $[48.5\\%, 50.5\\%]$\n\nThis interval estimates the range where the true proportion of ALL adults in the municipality likely falls.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice B: The word \"exactly\" is the giveaway. A sample can never tell you an exact population value -- it always comes with uncertainty. College Board puts this here because students sometimes think surveys give precise answers.\n- Choice C: This is the sneakiest trap. The interval is correct, but it says \"of the $271$ surveyed adults.\" We already KNOW exactly what the $519$ people said. The margin of error tells us about the POPULATION, not the sample.\n- Choice D: Future surveys will give different results due to sampling variability. The margin of error is not a prediction about future surveys.\n\n**Verification:** $65\\% - 4.5\\% = 44.5\\%$ and $55\\% + 4.5\\% = 69.5\\%$, applied to the population.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Margin of error = estimate plus-or-minus applied to the POPULATION. If the answer says \"sample\" or \"exactly\" or \"future surveys,\" it is wrong.",
