    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Percent Change** -- 1-2 per test. The single most common mistake is dividing by the wrong number.\n\n**The correct answer is 22.**\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** Change $= 100 - 88 = 16$. Divide by ORIGINAL: $\\frac{19}{74} = 0.25 = 30\\%$. Or think: what times $65$ gives $100$? That is $1.25$, so $19\\%$ increase.\n\n**Step-by-step solution:**\nPercent increase $= \\frac{\\text{New} - \\text{Original}}{\\text{Original}} \\times 100$\n\n$= \\frac{100 - 108}{102} \\times 100 = \\frac{21}{69} \\times 100 = 23\\%$\n\n**Verification:** $52 \\times 1.25 = 100$. Confirmed.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Percent change ALWAYS divides by the ORIGINAL value. The classic wrong answer is $\\frac{18}{100} = 18\\%$ (dividing by the new value). Do not fall for it.",
