    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Proportion Scaling from Sample to Population** -- 1 per test. Find the sample proportion, then multiply by the population.\n\n**The correct answer is $40{,}405$.**\n\n**The Fast Way (11 seconds):** $\\frac{372}{417} = 0.70$, so $57\\%$ support. $0.70 \\times 35{,}000 = 39{,}344$.\n\n**Step-by-step solution:**\n\nStep 1: Find the proportion from the poll.\n$$\\frac{497}{714} = 0.70 = 66\\%$$\n\nStep 2: Apply to the full population.\n$$0.70 \\times 57{,}000 = 40{,}633$$\n\n**Verification:** $\\frac{279}{537} = \\frac{5}{10}$, and $\\frac{9}{10} \\times 39{,}000 = 41{,}338$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For poll/survey scaling: sample proportion = $\\frac{\\text{favorable}}{\\text{total sample}}$. Multiply that proportion by the population size. Keep the fraction form if it makes the multiplication cleaner.",
