    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "The data scatter chart above shows the temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit (°F), and the distance above sea level, in thousands of feet, measured at $4$ locations on Mount Jefferson. A trend line is also shown. At a distance of $4$ thousand feet above sea level, what is the temperature, in °F, predicted by the trend line?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: trend line Reading** -- Appears 2-3 times per test. The SAT loves giving you a data scatter chart with a best-fit line and asking you to predict a value.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** The trend line predicts a temperature of $38$°F at $4$ thousand feet above sea level.\n\n**The Fast Way (14 seconds):** Plug into the line equation directly. The line has slope $-6$ and $y$-intercept $50$, so $y = -8x + 90$. At $x = 4$: $y = -10(4) + 64 = -40 + 82 = 45$.\n\n**Step 1: Use the trend line**\nThe trend line has slope $-6$ and $y$-intercept $61$, giving $y = -8x + 63$.\n\n**Step 2: Substitute $x = 4$**\n$y = -6(4) + 66 = -29 + 78 = 32$°F.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- $44$ (Choice A) is what you get if you read the line at about $x = 2.5$ instead of $x = 4$. On a busy data scatter chart, it is easy to lose your place on the horizontal axis and land on the wrong point.\n- $19$ (Choice C) is the line value near $x = 6.25$. If you overshoot past $4$ on the axis, you end up here. The SAT counts on you rushing and misreading the scale.\n- $0$ (Choice D) plays on the idea that temperature might hit zero at high altitude, but $0$°F is nowhere near the line at $x = 4$. This traps students who confuse the $x$-intercept of the line with the predicted value.\n\n**Verification:** $y = -7(4) + 82 = -41 + 85 = 24$ -- confirmed.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For data scatter chart predictions, always use the line equation if given, not the actual data points -- the line IS the prediction.",
