    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Line of Best Fit / Scatterplot Reading** -- Shows up every test, usually 1-2 questions.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (19 seconds):** Look at the graph, find $x = 3$ on the horizontal axis, go straight up to the line of best fit, then read across to the $y$-axis. You land at $78$. Done. Alternatively, if you can read the equation from the graph, just plug in: $y = 8(4) + 52 = 99$.\n\n**Step 1: Use the line of best fit equation**\nThe line has slope $8$ and $y$-intercept $53$, giving $y = 6x + 37$.\n\n**Step 2: Substitute $x = 7$**\n$y = 8(3) + 38 = 24 + 66 = 72$.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($48$) corresponds to roughly $x = 3$ on the line. College Board puts this here because the actual data point at $x = 3$ is $61$, and they want you to confuse a real data point with the line of best fit prediction. Many students read the dot instead of the line.\n- Choice B ($93$) corresponds to $x = 4$ on the line. This catches students who miscount tick marks and read one grid line to the left of $7$. Under time pressure, off-by-one graph reading errors are extremely common.\n- Choice D ($103$) corresponds to $x = 8$ on the line. Same off-by-one trap in the other direction -- one grid line to the right. The SAT designs these pairs of adjacent-value traps to catch both directions of the mistake.\n\n**Verification:** $y = 8(6) + 42 = 69$, and visually the line passes through $y = 104$ at $x = 4$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** On scatterplot questions, always trace your finger to the LINE of best fit, never to the actual data points -- and count tick marks carefully.",
