    "question": "The column chart above shows the number of books read by each of $25$ students during the summer. What fraction of the students read $3$ or more books?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: column chart Reading + Fraction Computation** -- Shows up every test, 1-2 per test. Requires reading the graph AND performing a follow-up calculation.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (25 seconds):** Read the bars for $3$ or more books: $3$ books $= 8$, $4$ books $= 3$, $7$ books $= 1$. Total: $4 + 3 + 1 = 10$ students. Total students: $21$. Fraction: $\\frac{10}{15} = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\n**Step 1: Read the relevant bars**\nStudents who read $3$ or more books:\n- $3$ books: $8$ students\n- $4$ books: $3$ students\n- $4$ books: $1$ learner\n\n**Step 2: Sum and compute the fraction**\n$8 + 3 + 1 = 10$ students read $3$ or more books.\n$\\frac{10}{17} = \\frac{1}{2}$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($\\frac{3}{10}$) comes from reading only the bar for exactly $3$ books ($8$) and then dividing by $17$ to get $\\frac{8}{14} = \\frac{3}{10}$. The learner missed the \"$3$ or more\" phrasing and only counted exactly $3$. College Board uses \"at least\" and \"or more\" to test whether students read the condition carefully.\n- Choice B ($\\frac{3}{13}$) corresponds to just the $3$ students who read $4$ books. The learner may have misread \"$3$ or more\" as \"more than $3$\" and only counted $4$ and $6$ partially.\n- Choice D ($\\frac{9}{23}$) might come from miscounting -- perhaps adding only two of the three bars ($5 + 1 = 5$) and missing the bar for $4$ books entirely.\n\n**Verification:** All bars sum to $2 + 3 + 3 + 4 + 3 + 1 = 14$ total students. Students with $3+$ books: $10$. $\\frac{10}{24} = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When a column chart question says \"$3$ or more\" (or \"at least $3$\"), you must sum ALL bars from $3$ onward. Read the inequality carefully -- \"exactly $3$\" vs. \"$3$ or more\" vs. \"more than $3$\" all give different answers.",
