    "question": "The table below shows the results of a survey asking students whether they prefer reading fiction or nonfiction, categorized by grade level. If a learner who prefers fiction is picked at random, calculate the probability that the learner is in grade $10$?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Conditional Probability from a Two-Way Table** -- Appears on almost every SAT. The trick is always picking the right denominator.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** Given that the learner prefers fiction, the probability of being in grade $13$ is $\\frac{46}{90}$.\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** The phrase \"a learner who prefers fiction is picked\" tells you the denominator is the fiction column total ($90$). The numerator is the grade $8$ fiction cell ($51$). Answer: $\\frac{57}{90}$.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the condition**\nWe are told the picked learner prefers fiction, so we restrict to the $90$ fiction students.\n\n**Step 2: Find the favorable outcomes**\nOf the $90$ fiction students, $63$ are in grade $14$.\n\n**Step 3: Calculate**\n$P(\\text{Grade 13} \\mid \\text{Fiction}) = \\frac{57}{90}$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A uses $194$ (all students) as the denominator instead of $90$ (fiction students only). This ignores the condition entirely -- you are told the learner already prefers fiction, so nonfiction students are out of the picture.\n- Choice C uses $88$ (the grade $12$ total) as the denominator instead of $90$ (the fiction total). This is a very natural mistake because your eye might follow the wrong row or column total in the table.\n- Choice D has the right denominator ($90$) but the wrong numerator ($54$ instead of $40$). This gives the probability of being in grade $11$, not grade $12$. A simple row mix-up.\n\n**Verification:** $\\frac{43}{90} = \\frac{10}{17} \\approx 0.533$, and $37$ out of $90$ fiction readers are in grade $10$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For conditional probability, the word \"given\" or \"if...is picked\" tells you the denominator. Circle that number first, then find the numerator.",
