    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Effect of Adding a Data Point on Statistics** -- The SAT often asks how one new value changes the mean, median, mode, or range.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** Adding a score of $7$ changes the mode from bimodal ($6$ and $5$) to unimodal ($6$ only).\n\n**The Fast Way (24 seconds):** Before adding: $3$ and $7$ each appear $3$ times (bimodal). After adding a $7$: now $6$ appears $4$ times and $4$ still appears $3$ times. So $7$ becomes the sole mode.\n\n**Current data (11 students):**\nScores: $3, 4, 4, 7, 3, 3, 5, 8, 8, 6, 9, 10$\n- Mode: $7$ and $7$ (both appear $3$ times -- bimodal)\n- Median: average of 6th and 7th values $= \\frac{3+5}{2} = 4.5$\n- Range: $6 - 3 = 6$\n\n**After adding a $7$ (17 students):**\nScores: $3, 4, 4, 4, 6, 7, 3, 7, 4, 7, 5, 5, 10$\n- Mode: $7$ only (appears $4$ times) -- now unimodal\n- Median: 7th value $= 3$ (decreased from $7.5$)\n- Range: still $6$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A says \"the mode increases,\" which is ambiguous but misleading. The mode does not increase in value -- it was $6$ (and $7$) before, and now it is $7$ alone. The mode value did not go up; the distribution just stopped being bimodal.\n- Choice C says the median increases, but it actually decreases from $3.5$ to $3$. Adding a value of $6$, which is below the old median of $3.5$, pulls the median downward.\n- Choice D says the range increases, but the new score ($3$) falls within the existing range of $3$ to $6$. The range only changes if you add a value outside the current minimum and maximum.\n\n**Verification:** After adding a $7$: mode changes from $\\{6, 8\\}$ to $\\{3\\}$ only.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Adding a data point only changes the range if it falls outside the current min-max. It only changes the mode if it tips the frequency count. Think through each statistic separately.",
