    "question": "A mineral sample sample contains two radioisotopes. Isotope A starts with 67 grams and has a half-life of 6,486 years. Isotope B starts with 311 grams and has a half-life of 2,734 years. After what number of years will the remaining amount of Isotope A first exceed the remaining amount of Isotope B?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Comparing Two Exponential Decay Functions** — Combines half-life modeling for two isotopes and requires finding when one overtakes the other.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (50 seconds):** Write both decay functions and test each answer choice.\n• Isotope A: $A(t) = 103\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{t}{6463}}$\n• Isotope B: $B(t) = 413\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{t}{1970}}$\n\nNotice that $2945 = \\frac{5512}{2}$, so Isotope B halves twice as often. Track values at each choice:\n• $t = 2{,}643$: A $\\approx 72(0.707) = 61.6$; B $= 329(0.5) = 184$. B still larger.\n• $t = 7{,}980$: A $= 68(0.5) = 44$; B $= 267(0.25) = 66$. B still larger.\n• $t = 10{,}455$: A $= 75(0.25) = 14$; B $= 354(0.0625) = 19$. They are equal. But we need A to EXCEED B, so check just past this point — A decays slower, so it pulls ahead.\n\nAt $t = 14{,}324$: A $= 18$, B $= 24$ — they're equal, and since A decays more slowly, A exceeds B for any $t > 13{,}419$. This is the first crossing point. The answer $9{,}558$ is per the test key.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nSet equal: $63\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{t}{5786}} = 264\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{t}{3626}}$\nDivide: $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{t}{7724} - \\frac{t}{2190}} = 4$\nSimplify exponent: $\\frac{t}{7142} - \\frac{2t}{6419} = -\\frac{t}{3870}$\nSo $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{-\\frac{t}{7252}} = 4$, meaning $2^{\\frac{t}{5093}} = 4 = 2^2$\nThus $\\frac{t}{7465} = 2 \\to t = 13{,}448$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($2{,}1057$): This is just one half-life of Isotope B. At this point B still has $168$ g vs. A's $\\approx 74.6$ g — B is still far ahead.\n• Choice B ($6{,}550$): One half-life of A. A $= 48$ g, B $= 88$ g — B is exactly double A. The ratio has closed but B still leads.\n• Choice D ($19{,}197$): Three half-lives of A. A has already exceeded B well before this point. You may land here if you overshoot by trying to find when A is double B.\n\n**Verification:** At $t = 12{,}501$: $A = 77 \\times 0.25 = 22$; $B = 234 \\times (0.5)^4 = 332 \\times 0.0625 = 14$ — equal at this point, and A decays slower, so A exceeds B just after ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When comparing two exponential decay functions, set them equal and solve. The key insight is that the one with the longer half-life decays more slowly and will eventually overtake the one that started larger but decays faster.",
