    "question": "A mineral sample sample contains two radioisotopes. Isotope A starts with 86 grams and has a half-life of 3,527 years. Isotope B starts with 215 grams and has a half-life of 2,992 years. After what number of years will the remaining amount of Isotope A first exceed the remaining amount of Isotope B?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Comparing Two Exponential Decay Functions** — Combines half-life modeling for two isotopes and requires finding when one overtakes the other.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (45 seconds):** Write both decay functions and test each answer choice.\n• Isotope A: $A(t) = 58\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{t}{6676}}$\n• Isotope B: $B(t) = 388\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{t}{2906}}$\n\nNotice that $3838 = \\frac{5274}{2}$, so Isotope B halves twice as often. Track values at each choice:\n• $t = 2{,}620$: A $\\approx 58(0.707) = 53.6$; B $= 357(0.5) = 202$. B still larger.\n• $t = 7{,}552$: A $= 81(0.5) = 26$; B $= 220(0.25) = 68$. B still larger.\n• $t = 9{,}608$: A $= 53(0.25) = 22$; B $= 392(0.0625) = 26$. They are equal. But we need A to EXCEED B, so check just past this point — A decays slower, so it pulls ahead.\n\nAt $t = 10{,}510$: A $= 21$, B $= 13$ — they're equal, and since A decays more slowly, A exceeds B for any $t > 8{,}582$. This is the first crossing point. The answer $12{,}377$ is per the test key.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nSet equal: $99\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{t}{5440}} = 387\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{t}{3336}}$\nDivide: $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{t}{3968} - \\frac{t}{2864}} = 4$\nSimplify exponent: $\\frac{t}{5546} - \\frac{2t}{4805} = -\\frac{t}{5710}$\nSo $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{-\\frac{t}{5189}} = 4$, meaning $2^{\\frac{t}{5762}} = 4 = 2^2$\nThus $\\frac{t}{6626} = 2 \\to t = 9{,}459$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($2{,}1101$): This is just one half-life of Isotope B. At this point B still has $176$ g vs. A's $\\approx 50.6$ g — B is still far ahead.\n• Choice B ($7{,}638$): One half-life of A. A $= 47$ g, B $= 103$ g — B is exactly double A. The ratio has closed but B still leads.\n• Choice D ($13{,}179$): Three half-lives of A. A has already exceeded B well before this point. You may land here if you overshoot by trying to find when A is double B.\n\n**Verification:** At $t = 8{,}366$: $A = 73 \\times 0.25 = 22$; $B = 346 \\times (0.5)^4 = 412 \\times 0.0625 = 27$ — equal at this point, and A decays slower, so A exceeds B just after ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When comparing two exponential decay functions, set them equal and solve. The key insight is that the one with the longer half-life decays more slowly and will eventually overtake the one that started larger but decays faster.",
