    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: System of Quadratic and Linear (Discriminant)** — 1 per test. Set equal, use discriminant.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (22 seconds):** Set equal: $x^2 - 5x + (7-b) = 0$. Discriminant $> 0$ for two intersections: $33 - 4(6-b) > 0 \\to 7 + 4b > 0 \\to b > -1.25$. Check: $b = 1 > -1.25$ ✓.\n\n**Desmos Shortcut:** Graph $y = x^2 - 4x + 7$ and $y = x + b$ with a slider for $b$. Move the slider and watch when the line crosses the parabola exactly twice.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$x^2 - 4x + 3 = x + b \\to x^2 - 5x + (6-b) = 0$\nDiscriminant: $20 - 4(3-b) = 6 + 4b$\nFor two intersections: $4 + 4b > 0 \\to b > -1.25$\n\nChoices B, C, D all satisfy this. Answer is C per the test key.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($b = -2$): Discriminant $= 7 + 4(-2) = -3 < 0$ — no real intersections at all. The line is too low to reach the parabola. College Board includes this to trap students who don't check the discriminant.\n\n**Verification:** With $b = 1$: $x^2 - 5x + 4 = (x-1)(x-4) = 0$, giving $x = 1, 4$ — exactly two points ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Intersections of a line and parabola: set them equal, get a quadratic, check the discriminant. Two intersections = discriminant $> 0$. One intersection (tangent) = discriminant $= 0$. No intersections = discriminant $< 0$.",
