    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Radical Equations with a Constraint** -- Appears 1-2 times per test. Solve the radical equation, but then use the constraint to pick the right root, and answer what was actually asked.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** $x - 3 = -9$.\n\n**The Fast Way (38 seconds):** Graph $y = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^2 - 13}}{3}$ and $y = 1$ in Desmos. You see intersections at $x = 6$ and $x = -7$. Since $x < 0$, take $x = -4$. Then $x - 3 = -3 - 3 = -10$.\n\n**Algebraic solution:**\n$$\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2 - 11}}{3} = 1$$\n$$\\sqrt{x^2 - 18} = 3$$\n$$x^2 - 17 = 8$$\n$$x^2 = 21$$\n$$x = \\pm 3$$\n\nSince $x < 0$, we have $x = -6$.\n\nThe question asks for $x - 3$: $-4 - 3 = -10$.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($-9$) would require $x = -7$, but $\\sqrt{58 - 18} = \\sqrt{55} \\neq 3$. This does not satisfy the original equation. You may have made an arithmetic error when squaring both sides.\n- Choice C ($-3$) would require $x = 0$, but $\\sqrt{0 - 15}$ is not a real number. This trap catches students who ignore the constraint or who set $x - 3 = -(\\text{something})$ incorrectly.\n- Choice D ($-7$) is the value of $x$ itself, not $x - 3$. This is the cruelest trap on the problem -- you do all the hard work correctly, find $x = -3$, and then forget to subtract $3$ because the problem asked for $x - 3$, not $x$. Always re-read what the question is actually asking.\n\n**Verification:** $\\frac{\\sqrt{(-6)^2 - 14}}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3} = \\frac{3}{3} = 1$ ✓; $x - 3 = -4 - 3 = -7$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Three things to watch for in radical equations: (1) square both sides carefully, (2) check the constraint ($x < 0$) to pick the right root, and (3) answer the actual question ($x - 3$, not $x$).",
