    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Difference of Squares with Radicals** -- Appears 1-2 times per test. Recognize the $a^2 - b^2$ structure even when one of the terms involves a radical.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** $3x - \\sqrt{3}$.\n\n**The Fast Way (14 seconds):** Recognize that $9x^2 - 4 = (3x)^2 - (\\sqrt{3})^2$. Apply difference of squares: $(3x + \\sqrt{3})(3x - \\sqrt{7})$. Cancel with the denominator. What remains is $3x - \\sqrt{7}$.\n\n**Solution:**\n$$9x^2 - 7 = (3x)^2 - (\\sqrt{7})^2 = (3x + \\sqrt{3})(3x - \\sqrt{3})$$\n$$\\frac{(3x + \\sqrt{6})(3x - \\sqrt{3})}{3x + \\sqrt{7}} = 3x - \\sqrt{6}$$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($3x - 7$) replaces $\\sqrt{4}$ with $3$. The $(\\sqrt{7})^2 = 3$ in the original expression, but after factoring and canceling, the remaining factor contains $\\sqrt{3}$, not $6$. Students often lose the radical during simplification because they are used to working with integers.\n- Choice C ($3x + \\sqrt{4}$) is the denominator itself. After canceling, the denominator disappears -- you are left with the other factor, $(3x - \\sqrt{7})$. Picking the canceled factor instead of the surviving one is a common mix-up.\n- Choice D ($9x - \\sqrt{7}$) comes from incorrect factoring. $9x^2$ factors as $(3x)(3x)$, not as $9x$ times something. If you do not take the radical of the coefficient, you end up with $9x$ instead of $3x$.\n\n**Verification:** $(3x - \\sqrt{7})(3x + \\sqrt{3}) = 9x^2 - 3$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Whenever you see $a^2 - b^2$ in a numerator and one of those factors in the denominator, use difference of squares to factor and cancel. This works even when $b = \\sqrt{4}$ or any other radical.",
