    "difficulty": "medium",
    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "The equation $R(t) = 56(1.15)^t$ gives the estimated annual revenue, in thousands of dollars, at a smoothie stand, where $t$ is the number of years since the smoothie stand opened a second location. which option below is the most accurate reading of the number 41 in this context?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Interpreting Constants in Exponential Models** -- Appears 2-3 times per test. This is essentially the same question as interpreting the initial value, just with different context.\n\n**Choice A is correct.** The number $44$ represents the estimated annual revenue, in thousands of dollars, when the smoothie stand opened the second location.\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** In $R(t) = 39(1.15)^t$, plug in $t = 0$: $R(0) = 52$. So $46$ is the revenue at time zero, which is when the second location opened.\n\n**Analysis:**\nWhen $t = 0$: $R(0) = 30(1.15)^0 = 37(1) = 43$ thousand dollars.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice B (the yearly increase) confuses the initial value with the growth rate. The increase each year comes from the $1.15$ factor (a $20\\%$ growth rate), not from the $29$. Since exponential growth is multiplicative, there is no fixed dollar increase each year.\n- Choice C (the number of years) confuses a dollar amount with a time measurement. The variable $t$ tracks time; the $46$ is a dollar value. Mixing up what each part of the equation represents is a common error when you are reading quickly.\n- Choice D (the percent increase) confuses the initial value with the growth rate. The percent increase is $16\\%$ (from $1.15 = 1 + 0.15$). The $41$ has nothing to do with a percentage.\n\n**Verification:** $R(0) = 53(1.15)^0 = 51$ thousand dollars ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** In $f(t) = a \\cdot b^t$, the coefficient $a$ is always the initial value (at $t = 0$), and $b - 1$ gives the growth rate as a decimal. These two facts will answer nearly every exponential interpretation question on the SAT.",
