    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "In triangle $PQR$, the measure of angle $Q$ is $52°$ and the measure of the external angle at vertex $R$ is $84°$. Side $QR$ is extended through $R$ to a point $S$, forming triangle $PRS$. If $\\angle PRS = 136°$, find the measure of angle $\\angle PSR$, in degrees?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Exterior Angle Theorem Applied Across Two Triangles** -- This pattern chains the exterior angle theorem with the triangle angle sum, requiring you to find an angle in the original triangle first and then use it in an adjacent triangle.\n\n**Choice A is correct.** $\\angle PSR = 7°$.\n\n**The Fast Way (22 seconds):** internal angle $R$ in triangle $PQR$: $180° - 144° = 86°$. Angle $P$: $180° - 47° - 55° = 76°$. In triangle $PRS$, $\\angle PRS = 78°$ (the exterior angle at $R$) and $\\angle RPS = \\angle P = 55°$. But wait -- $\\angle RPS$ is the same as $\\angle P$ in triangle $PQR$ only if $S$ is on line $QR$. Since $\\angle PRS = 138°$ and the angles of triangle $PRS$ must sum to $180°$: $\\angle PSR = 180° - 105° - 48° = 4°$.\n\n**Solution steps:**\n1. Find internal angle $R$: $R = 180° - 105° = 53°$\n2. Find angle $P$ in triangle $PQR$: $P = 180° - 39° - 75° = 67°$\n3. In triangle $PRS$: $\\angle PRS = 119°$ and $\\angle RPS = 88°$ (same vertex $P$)\n4. $\\angle PSR = 180° - 74° - 74° = 8°$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- **B ($29°$):** This is angle $Q$ from the original triangle. You might assume the new triangle inherits the same angles, but triangle $PRS$ has a completely different shape.\n- **C ($83°$):** This is angle $P$ from the original triangle. You found it correctly but answered with the intermediate result instead of the final angle $\\angle PSR$ that was asked for.\n- **D ($65°$):** This is the internal angle $R$ in the original triangle. You stopped at the first conversion step and mistakenly thought this was the answer.\n\n**Verification:** $111° + 70° + 4° = 180°$, confirming the angles of triangle $PRS$ sum correctly.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When a problem extends a side to create a new triangle, find all angles in the original triangle first. Then use those angles (especially at shared vertices) to set up the angle sum in the new triangle.",
