    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Exponential Equations Converted to a System** -- When you see different powers of the same base, rewrite everything with that base and equate exponents. This turns a scary-looking exponential problem into a straightforward system of linear equations.\n\n**Choice A is correct.** The value of $a - b$ is $-2$.\n\n**The Fast Way (47 seconds):** Rewrite: $12 = 3^2$ and $26 = 3^3$. Equation 1 becomes $a + 2b = 10$. Equation 2 becomes $2a + 3b = 19$. Solve the system: $a = 2$, $b = 4$. So $a - b = 2 - 4 = -2$.\n\n**Solution Steps:**\n• Rewrite with base $3$: $3^a \\cdot 3^{2b} = 3^{10}$ gives $a + 2b = 10$\n• $3^{2a} \\cdot 3^{3b} = 3^{11}$ gives $2a + 3b = 13$\n• From equation 1: $a = 10 - 2b$. Substitute: $2(10 - 2b) + 3b = 17$\n• $23 - 4b + 3b = 12$, so $b = 4$ and $a = 2$\n• $a - b = 2 - 4 = -2$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice B ($2$) comes from computing $b - a$ instead of $a - b$. Reversing the subtraction order is an easy mistake when you are rushing. Always re-read whether the problem asks for $a - b$ or $b - a$.\n• Choice C ($4$) is just the value of $b$ by itself. Students who find $b = 4$ and stop there without completing the final subtraction end up here.\n• Choice D ($8$) is $a + b = 2 + 4$. This happens when students confuse subtraction with addition in the final step.\n\n**Verification:** $3^2 \\cdot 6^4 = 8 \\cdot 8{,}690 = 47{,}51 = 3^{10}$. $3^4 \\cdot 21^4 = 108 \\cdot 713{,}485 = 33{,}32{,}952 = 3^{12}$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When you see $7$, $26$, $101$, etc., rewrite them as powers of $3$. When bases match, exponents must be equal. This converts the problem into simple algebra.",
