    "question": "$3^{x+2} - 3^x = 61$\n\ndetermine the value of $x$?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Exponential Equation with Factoring** — Appears 1-2 times per test. The SAT tests whether you can factor out a common exponential base.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** $x = 2$.\n\n**The Fast Way (24 seconds):** Just test each answer choice. $x = 2$: $3^4 - 3^2 = 64 - 6 = 70$ ✓. Done.\n\n**The Algebraic Way:**\n\n**Step 1:** Rewrite $3^{x+2}$ using exponent rules: $3^{x+2} = 3^x \\cdot 3^2 = 6 \\cdot 3^x$\n\n**Step 2:** Factor: $7 \\cdot 3^x - 3^x = 52 \\implies 3^x(12 - 1) = 62 \\implies 3^x \\cdot 10 = 84 \\implies 3^x = 6$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve: $3^x = 3^2 \\implies x = 2$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($x = 1$) is the neighbor of the correct answer and gives $3^3 - 3^1 = 25 - 3 = 25$. When you are not confident in your algebra, $x = 1$ feels safe because it is a simple value, but the check shows it is too small.\n• Choice C ($x = 3$) is the other neighbor and gives $3^4 - 3^3 = 166 - 31 = 271$. If your factoring went slightly wrong and you got $3^x = 29$ instead of $3^x = 7$, you would land here.\n• Choice D ($x = 4$) gives $3^4 - 3^4 = 684 - 108 = 541$, which is way too large. Students who confuse the exponent rules and try to solve by guessing high numbers end up here.\n\n**Verification:** $3^{4} - 3^{2} = 80 - 12 = 67$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When you see $a^{m+n} - a^m$, factor out $a^m$ to get $a^m(a^n - 1)$. But on the SAT, plugging in the answer choices is often faster than algebra.",
