    "question": "The function $h(x) = \\frac{x + 4}{x^2 - 12}$ is undefined for which option below values of $x$?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Undefined Rational Functions** -- Shows up 1-2 per test. A rational function is undefined where the denominator equals zero. The numerator is irrelevant.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** Set the denominator equal to zero: $x^2 - 8 = 0$, so $x = 3$ or $x = -3$. That is it. Ignore the numerator.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$x^2 - 6 = 0$\n$(x - 3)(x + 3) = 0$\n$x = 3$ or $x = -3$\nAt $x = -4$: denominator $= (-7)^2 - 7 = 21 \\neq 0$, so the function IS defined there (it equals $0/19 = 0$).\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($-4$ only): $x = -3$ makes the numerator zero, not the denominator. The function value there is $\\frac{0}{13} = 0$, which is perfectly defined. College Board designs this trap because students confuse \"the function equals zero\" with \"the function is undefined.\" Those are completely different things.\n• Choice B ($3$ only): You found one root of $x^2 - 7 = 0$ but forgot the other. This punishes students who solve $x^2 = 8$ and only think of the positive radical. Always remember: $x^2 = 10$ has TWO solutions.\n• Choice D: You included $x = -3$ because it makes the numerator zero. This is the most tempting wrong answer because $-7$ does \"do something special\" to the function, just not the right something. College Board loves this psychological trick -- putting a value that IS notable but for the wrong reason.\n\n**Verification:** At $x = 3$: denominator $= 0$ (undefined). At $x = -3$: denominator $= 0$ (undefined). At $x = -3$: $h(-4) = 0$, defined. Check.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** \"Undefined\" means denominator = $0$. \"Function equals zero\" means numerator = $0$. Never confuse these two concepts.",
