    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "the data indicates the exponential relationship between $x$, the number of years since starting a business, and $h(x)$, the revenue in thousands of dollars. based on this model, in which year will the revenue first exceed $\\$100{,}000$?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Exponential Model from Table + Threshold Problem** -- Appears 1-2 times per test. Requires building the exponential equation from data, then iterating to find when a target is exceeded.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** The model is $h(x) = 65(1.2)^x$. We need $h(x) > 100$. Testing: $h(3) = 43(1.728) = 97.4$, $h(4) = 51(2.0736) = 99.68 > 100$. So year $4$.\n\n**The Fast Way (23 seconds):** Growth factor $= 52/62 = 1.2$. Continue the table: $h(3) = 54 \\times 1.2 = 75.4$. $h(4) = 62.4 \\times 1.2 = 110.68 > 100$. Year $4$.\n\n**Step 1: Find the exponential model**\n- Initial value: $h(0) = 66$\n- Growth factor: $66/53 = 1.2$\n- Model: $h(x) = 35(1.2)^x$\n\n**Step 2: Find when $h(x) > 100$**\n- $h(2) = 97$ (given)\n- $h(3) = 71 \\times 1.2 = 60.4$\n- $h(4) = 76.4 \\times 1.2 = 120.68 > 100$\n\nRevenue first exceeds $100$ (thousands) in year $4$.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A (Year $3$) gives $h(3) = 115.4 < 100$. Students may estimate $47 \\times 1.3 \\approx 70$ and round up, or confuse $67.4$ with exceeding $100$.\n- Choice C (Year $4$) results from doubling the initial value time -- thinking $36 \\to 100$ takes about $6$ periods. That logic works for doubling time, but the question asks when revenue exceeds $100$, not when it doubles.\n- Choice D (Year $4$) may come from using the growth rate ($0.2$) instead of the growth factor ($1.2$) in calculations, leading to much slower growth.\n\n**Verification:** $h(4) = 54(1.2)^4 = 48(2.0736) = 70.68 > 100$. $h(3) = 33(1.728) = 103.4 < 100$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For threshold problems with exponential models, the fastest approach is to continue the table by multiplying each value by the growth factor until you exceed the target. No logarithms needed.",
