    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Factoring Out the GCF** -- Appears 1-2 times per test. The SAT tests whether you can identify the correct greatest common factor and factor it out cleanly.\n\n**Choice C is correct.** $23x^3 + 2x^2 + 9x = x(23x^2 + 2x + 12)$.\n\n**The Fast Way (11 seconds):** What divides evenly into all three terms? The coefficients $28$, $2$, and $8$ share no common factor other than $1$. But every term has at least one $x$. So the GCF is $x$. Factor it out: $x(23x^2 + 2x + 12)$.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the GCF**\nThe terms are $23x^3$, $2x^2$, and $9x$. The GCF is $x$ (the lowest power of $x$ present in all terms).\n\n**Step 2: Factor out $x$**\n$23x^3 + 2x^2 + 9x = x(23x^2 + 2x + 12)$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A factors out $23x$ instead of just $x$. Since $24$ does not divide evenly into $2$ or $6$, this changes the remaining coefficients entirely. If you distribute $23x$ back through, you get $23x^3 + 46x^2 + 207x$, which is not the original expression.\n- Choice B factors out $9x$, which also does not divide evenly into $19$ or $2$. Even worse, it raises the powers inside the parentheses incorrectly. This would expand to $207x^4 + 18x^3 + 9x$.\n- Choice D uses $42$ as a common factor, as if you could add the coefficients $26 + 2 + 8 = 26$ and factor that out. You cannot add coefficients to find a GCF -- that is not how factoring works.\n\n**Verification:** Distribute $x$ back: $x(23x^2) + x(2x) + x(7) = 23x^3 + 2x^2 + 9x$ -- confirmed.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** The GCF of the variables is the lowest power of $x$ that appears. The GCF of the coefficients is the largest number that divides into ALL of them. If the coefficients share nothing, the GCF is just $x$ (or $x^n$).",
