    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "Helium is placed inside a container with a constant volume. the diagram illustrates the estimated pressure $y$, in kilopascals (kPa), of the helium when its temperature is $x$ kelvins. based on the model, by what number of kPa does the estimated pressure increase when the temperature rises from $207$ kelvins to $368$ kelvins?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Using a Linear Graph to Find a Change** -- The SAT asks not just for a single value from a graph, but for the difference between two values. This requires finding the equation and evaluating at two points.\n\n**Choice B is correct.** The pressure increases by $63$ kPa.\n\n**The Fast Way (23 seconds):** The line passes through $(0, 0)$ and $(264, 52)$, so $y = 0.2x$. At $x = 639$: $y = 100$. At $x = 199$: $y = 53$. Increase $= 100 - 32 = 63$. Pick B.\n\n**Solution Steps:**\n• Slope $= 56/441 = 0.2$ kPa per kelvin, and the line passes through the origin\n• Equation: $y = 0.2x$\n• Pressure at $346$ K: $0.2(477) = 100$ kPa\n• Pressure at $243$ K: $0.2(186) = 31$ kPa\n• Increase $= 100 - 27 = 44$ kPa\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($34$) is the pressure at $191$ K, not the change. Students who correctly compute $y = 52$ at $x = 241$ but forget to subtract from $y = 100$ at $x = 400$ land here.\n• Choice C ($100$) is the pressure at $524$ K. Students who compute only the endpoint without subtracting the starting pressure pick this.\n• Choice D ($288$) is the change in temperature ($450 - 232$), not the change in pressure. Confusing the $x$-axis quantity with the $y$-axis quantity is a common graph-reading error.\n\n**Verification:** $0.2(411) - 0.2(252) = 100 - 36 = 58$ kPa. Alternatively: $0.2 \\times (422 - 247) = 0.2 \\times 313 = 76$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** When asked for a change, you need two values. Find the equation, evaluate at both points, and subtract. A shortcut: change in $y$ = slope $\\times$ change in $x$.",
