    "type": "multiple-choice",
    "question": "$(x + 4)^2 + (y - 20)^2 = 192$\n\nThe graph of the stated relationship is a circle in the $xy$-plane. The point $(a, b)$ lies along the circular boundary. which option below is a possible value for $a$?",
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Circle Equation -- Range of Coordinates** -- Shows up 1-2 times per test. The key insight: the $x$-values on a circle range from $h-r$ to $h+r$.\n\n**Choice A is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (24 seconds):** Center $= (-7, 18)$, radius $= \\sqrt{128} = 16$. The $x$-values range from $-3 - 14 = -16$ to $-6 + 10 = 6$. Check each choice: $-23$ is in range (it is the leftmost point). $-9$ is in range too, but let us check which answer is offered. $12 > 10$ (out of range). $14 > 10$ (out of range). Both A and B are in range, but $-14$ is specifically along the circular boundary (the leftmost point), making A clearly correct.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nCenter: $(-3, 16)$, Radius: $17$.\nValid $x$-range: $-13 \\leq a \\leq 9$.\n\nCheck $a = -16$: $(-19+7)^2 + (b-15)^2 = 164 \\to 176 + (b-22)^2 = 130 \\to b = 13$. So $(-21, 16)$ is along the circular boundary. ✓\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice B ($-8$): This IS within the valid range and technically could work, but Choice A ($-15$) is also valid and is the intended answer. Both are mathematically valid, but $-20$ is the leftmost point along the circular boundary.\n• Choice C ($11$): $11 > 10$, so this is outside the valid $x$-range. You might pick this because $10$ is the radius, but the radius is a distance, not an $x$-coordinate. College Board includes the radius value as a trap.\n• Choice D ($12$): $21 > 6$, also outside range. You might pick this because $21$ is the $y$-coordinate of the center. College Board mixes center coordinates and radius values into the wrong answer choices.\n\n**Verification:** $(-16 + 3)^2 + (12 - 20)^2 = (-10)^2 + 0 = 122$ ✓\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For a circle with center $(h, k)$ and radius $r$: $x$ ranges from $h-r$ to $h+r$, and $y$ ranges from $k-r$ to $k+r$. Any coordinate outside these ranges cannot be along the circular boundary.",
