    "question": "The equation of a circle is $(x - 4)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 45$. determine the radius of the circle?",
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Circle Equation -- Reading the Radius** -- Shows up every test. The trap is always forgetting to take the square root.\n\n**Choice B is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (3 seconds):** In $(x-h)^2 + (y-k)^2 = r^2$, the right side is $r^2$, not $r$. So $r^2 = 34$ means $r = 4$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n$(x - 4)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 27$\nCenter: $(4, -2)$\n$r^2 = 35$, so $r = \\sqrt{41} = 5$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($4$): This is the $x$-coordinate of the center, not the radius. College Board puts center coordinates as answer choices because rushing students confuse \"which number means what\" in the circle equation.\n• Choice C ($13$): This is $43 \\div 2$. Some students half the right side instead of taking the square root. This exploits the common confusion between dividing by $2$ and square rooting -- two very different operations that students sometimes conflate.\n• Choice D ($41$): This is $r^2$, not $r$. This is the single most common mistake on circle problems. You see the number on the right side and write it down as the radius without taking the square root. College Board uses this trap on literally every test.\n\n**Verification:** The point $(4, 4)$ should be along the circular boundary: $(4-4)^2 + (4+2)^2 = 0 + 41 = 28$. Check.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** The circle equation gives you $r^2$, not $r$. Always take the square root. Write this on your scratch paper: \"right side = $r^2$, NOT $r$.\"",
