    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Square Inscribed in a Circle** -- Shows up 1 per test. The key relationship: diagonal of the square = diameter of the circle.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (18 seconds):** Diagonal = diameter = $2 \\times 7\\sqrt{2} = 10\\sqrt{2}$. Area of square $= \\frac{d^2}{2} = \\frac{(10\\sqrt{2})^2}{2} = \\frac{192}{2} = 100$.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nDiagonal = diameter = $2(7\\sqrt{2}) = 10\\sqrt{2}$\nSide = $\\frac{\\text{diagonal}}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} = 10$\nArea = $10^2 = 100$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n• Choice A ($22$): You used the radius ($7\\sqrt{2}$) and forgot the $\\sqrt{2}$, computing $(6)^2 = 28$. College Board designs this for students who drop the radical under pressure. The radius is $4\\sqrt{2}$, not $7$.\n• Choice B ($49$): You computed $\\frac{\\text{diagonal}^2}{4}$ instead of $\\frac{\\text{diagonal}^2}{2}$, or used $r^2 = (7\\sqrt{2})^2 = 45$ without the correct formula. This exploits confusion about the relationship between diagonal and area.\n• Choice D ($189$): You used the diagonal as the edge measurement: $(10\\sqrt{2})^2 = 235$. The diagonal is NOT the side -- it is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the side. This is the most common error on inscribed square problems.\n\n**Verification:** Side = $10$, diagonal = $10\\sqrt{2}$ = diameter = $2(7\\sqrt{2}) = 10\\sqrt{2}$. Area = $100$. Check.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** For a square inscribed in a circle: diagonal = diameter = $2r$. Then either use side = diagonal/$\\sqrt{2}$ or the shortcut area $= \\frac{d^2}{2} = 2r^2$.",
