    "question": "In triangle $DEF$, the measure of angle $D$ is $58°$ and the measure of angle $E$ is $54°$. Side $\\overline{EF}$ is extended through $F$ to a point $G$, forming external angle $\\angle DFG$. find the measure, in degrees, of the external angle $\\angle DFG$?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Exterior Angle Theorem + Triangle Angle Sum** -- Shows up 1 per test. Combines two geometry concepts.\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (20 seconds):** By the Exterior Angle Theorem, an exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two non-adjacent interior angles. So $\\angle DFG = \\angle D + \\angle E = 44° + 82° = 164°$.\n\n**Method 2 (Multi-step approach):**\n\n**Step 1: Find the internal angle $\\angle F$**\n$\\angle F = 180° - 50° - 90° = 57°$\n\n**Step 2: Find the exterior angle**\nSince $\\angle DFG$ and $\\angle DFE$ (which is $\\angle F$) form a linear pair:\n$\\angle DFG = 180° - 64° = 103°$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($61°$) is the internal angle $\\angle F$ itself, not the exterior angle. Students who correctly find $\\angle F = 57°$ but forget that the question asks for the EXTERIOR angle pick this. College Board counts on students not re-reading what is actually being asked.\n- Choice B ($77°$) might come from computing $180° - 84° = 78°$, using only one angle instead of both non-adjacent angles. This shows a partial application of the exterior angle theorem.\n- Choice D ($127°$) could come from $180° - 67° = 107°$, again using only one of the two non-adjacent angles.\n\n**Verification:** Exterior angle $= 60° + 55° = 86°$, and $100° + 54° = 180°$ (linear pair). Both methods confirm the answer.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** The Exterior Angle Theorem is a powerful shortcut: exterior angle = sum of the two remote interior angles. It saves a step compared to finding the internal angle first and then subtracting from $180°$.",
