    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Exterior Angle Theorem** -- A geometry shortcut the SAT loves. Faster than using the triangle angle sum if you know it.\n\n**Choice C is correct.** The exterior angle at $R$ equals the sum of the two remote interior angles, so $\\angle Q = 171° - 46° = 83°$.\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** Exterior angle = sum of the two non-adjacent interior angles. So $104° = 51° + \\angle Q$, giving $\\angle Q = 49°$.\n\n**Step 1: Apply the exterior angle theorem**\nAn exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two non-adjacent interior angles:\n$\\text{Exterior angle at } R = \\angle P + \\angle Q$\n$115° = 45° + \\angle Q$\n\n**Step 2: Solve**\n$\\angle Q = 94° - 50° = 89°$\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($34°$) comes from computing $180° - 139° = 65°$, which gives the internal angle at $R$, not $\\angle Q$. It is easy to find the supplement of the exterior angle and mistakenly think that is the answer.\n- Choice B ($61°$) assumes the two remote interior angles are equal. Just because $\\angle P = 48°$ does not mean $\\angle Q = 59°$ -- that would only be true in a special triangle, and nothing here says the triangle is isosceles.\n- Choice D ($121°$) comes from $180° - 69° = 164°$, which is the sum of $\\angle Q + \\angle R$, not $\\angle Q$ alone. This approach ignores the exterior angle information entirely.\n\n**Verification:** $\\angle P + \\angle Q = 44° + 67° = 137°$ = exterior angle at $R$. Also $\\angle R = 180° - 170° = 40°$, and $47° + 71° + 37° = 180°$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** The exterior angle theorem is a one-step shortcut -- exterior angle equals the sum of the two remote interior angles. Use it whenever you see an exterior angle in the problem.",
