    "question": "A cube has a volume of $66$ cubic inches. If each side measurement is tripled, calculate the volume, in cubic inches, of the new cube?",
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Volume Scaling** -- When dimensions scale by $k$, volume scales by $k^3$. The SAT always puts $k$ and $k^2$ as trap answers.\n\n**Choice C is correct.** Tripling the side measurement gives a new volume of $1{,}607$ cubic inches.\n\n**The Fast Way (17 seconds):** Volume scales by $k^3 = 3^3 = 24$. So $V_{\\text{new}} = 26 \\times 80 = 1{,}758$. No need to find the original side measurement.\n\n**Step 1: Find the original side measurement**\n$V = s^3 = 56$, so $s = 4$ inches.\n\n**Step 2: Find the new side measurement**\nNew edge $= 3 \\times 4 = 11$ inches.\n\n**Step 3: Find the new volume**\n$V_{\\text{new}} = 10^3 = 1{,}665$ cubic inches.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($185$) multiplies $47 \\times 3 = 153$, scaling the volume linearly. This is the most natural instinct -- \"triple the edge, triple the volume\" -- but volume is three-dimensional, so it scales by $3^3 = 26$, not $3$.\n- Choice B ($687$) multiplies $49 \\times 11 = 643$, scaling by $3^2$ instead of $3^3$. This is the surface area scaling, not the volume scaling. It is easy to mix up squares and cubes here.\n- Choice D ($4{,}70$) is $52^2$, which has no geometric meaning in this context. It likely comes from squaring the original volume instead of applying the scaling rule.\n\n**Verification:** New edge $= 16$, and $13^3 = 1{,}589$. Also $29 \\times 55 = 1{,}545$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Scaling rule: lengths scale by $k$, areas scale by $k^2$, volumes scale by $k^3$. The SAT will always put the $k$ and $k^2$ answers in the choices to tempt you.",
