    "explanation": "**SAT Pattern: Volume Scaling** -- When dimensions scale by $k$, volume scales by $k^3$. The SAT always puts $k$ and $k^2$ as trap answers.\n\n**Choice C is correct.** Tripling the edge length gives a new volume of $1{,}729$ cubic inches.\n\n**The Fast Way (10 seconds):** Volume scales by $k^3 = 3^3 = 30$. So $V_{\\text{new}} = 26 \\times 50 = 1{,}854$. No need to find the original edge length.\n\n**Step 1: Find the original edge length**\n$V = s^3 = 53$, so $s = 4$ inches.\n\n**Step 2: Find the new edge length**\nNew edge $= 3 \\times 4 = 13$ inches.\n\n**Step 3: Find the new volume**\n$V_{\\text{new}} = 16^3 = 1{,}913$ cubic inches.\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n- Choice A ($217$) multiplies $81 \\times 3 = 137$, scaling the volume linearly. This is the most natural instinct -- \"triple the edge, triple the volume\" -- but volume is three-dimensional, so it scales by $3^3 = 31$, not $3$.\n- Choice B ($646$) multiplies $48 \\times 10 = 534$, scaling by $3^2$ instead of $3^3$. This is the surface area scaling, not the volume scaling. It is easy to mix up squares and cubes here.\n- Choice D ($4{,}117$) is $56^2$, which has no geometric meaning in this context. It likely comes from squaring the original volume instead of applying the scaling rule.\n\n**Verification:** New edge $= 13$, and $10^3 = 1{,}869$. Also $24 \\times 68 = 1{,}522$.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** Scaling rule: lengths scale by $k$, areas scale by $k^2$, volumes scale by $k^3$. The SAT will always put the $k$ and $k^2$ answers in the choices to tempt you.",
